// Original explanations and interview scenarios. Keys match the public topic inventory.
// Each trace is an authored, illustrative sequence, not an execution engine.
const T = (concept, scenario, trace, mistake, question, answer) => ({ concept, scenario, trace, mistake, question, answer });
export const osRoadmapContent = {
  "what-is-an-operating-system": T(
    "An operating system multiplexes hardware and enforces boundaries between programs. CPU scheduling virtualizes execution; virtual memory isolates addresses; files provide persistent naming; drivers translate generic operations into device commands. Policy decides who receives a resource, while mechanism implements that decision.",
    "Two editors save files while a video plays. Explain why one application cannot directly overwrite the other's memory, and why all three can progress on one CPU.",
    "Editor|Requests write through a syscall|User mode;Kernel|Validates descriptor and schedules I/O|Protected resource;Scheduler|Runs video while storage waits|CPU remains useful",
    "An OS provides isolation, not unlimited capacity. A correct application can still experience contention and resource exhaustion.",
    "Why is a file API more useful than exposing disk sectors?", "The API hides device details and centralizes naming, permissions, allocation, caching, and recovery. Applications still need explicit durability decisions."),
  "what-happens-when-you-run-a-program": T(
    "A shell starts a process, and an exec operation replaces its program image. The loader maps executable segments, establishes a stack and runtime metadata, and transfers control to startup code. Dynamic linking may resolve shared libraries. Pages can be populated lazily as execution touches them.",
    "Trace launching a small command from a shell. Distinguish creating a child from replacing the child's image, and distinguish mapped memory from resident memory.",
    "Shell|Creates child process|Separate PID;Loader|Maps executable and libraries|New address space;CPU|Executes startup then main|Demand faults may occur",
    "exec does not create a second PID, and mapping an executable does not mean every byte has already been read into RAM.",
    "Why can the first request be slower than later requests?", "Startup, dynamic linking, page faults, and cold instruction/data caches add work before the steady-state path is warm."),
  "course-environment": T(
    "Use a disposable Linux VM or WSL environment for Linux-specific experiments. Record the kernel, architecture, compiler, and resource limits so observations are reproducible. Compile small programs with warnings and debug symbols, then compare process, memory, and syscall observations against a prediction.",
    "Prepare a hello-world process and inspect it without modifying host-wide settings. Commands are local experiments: uname -a, cc --version, ulimit -n, and ps.",
    "Environment|Record uname and compiler|Platform assumptions;Build|cc -Wall -Wextra -g hello.c|Check diagnostics;Observe|Run and inspect with ps|Compare PID and exit status",
    "Linux, WSL, containers, and macOS do not expose identical kernels or tools. A container cannot emulate every host feature.",
    "What belongs in a reproducible OS bug report?", "Include a minimal program, exact commands, kernel/runtime versions, limits, expected versus observed behavior, and a bounded trace."),
  "c-survival-guide-for-os": T(
    "C exposes pointers, object lifetimes, byte counts, and manual allocation directly. A pointer is not its allocation length. Check syscall return values, use size_t for sizes and ssize_t for signed byte-count results, and distinguish errno after a failed call from stale errno after success. Ownership must identify who frees each allocation.",
    "A read into a 16-byte buffer returns 5. Process exactly 5 bytes; the buffer is not automatically a null-terminated string. Retry interrupted operations only when their contract permits.",
    "Allocate|Capacity 16 bytes|Ownership assigned;Read|Return value 5|Only five bytes valid;Consume|Use explicit byte count|Free once after last use",
    "strlen on arbitrary input can read past the valid buffer. Use-after-free and double-free are undefined behavior, not predictable exceptions.",
    "Why is sizeof(pointer) not the buffer capacity?", "It measures the pointer representation. Carry the allocation length separately and check arithmetic before allocating or indexing."),
  "essential-system-tools": T(
    "Choose tools by the question: ps and top expose tasks, vmstat summarizes runnable and blocked work, free reports memory categories, iostat describes devices, ss describes sockets, and strace records syscalls. Collect time-aligned samples and interpret rates rather than treating one cumulative counter as a diagnosis.",
    "An API is slow with low CPU utilization. Compare runnable tasks, blocked tasks, storage latency, and syscall durations before increasing CPU resources.",
    "top|CPU mostly idle|CPU saturation unlikely;vmstat|Blocked tasks increase|Investigate waiting;strace|Long storage calls|Test disk hypothesis",
    "A tracing tool changes timing and may require privileges. Collect a narrow sample and correlate it with user-visible latency.",
    "Why is low CPU not proof that the server is healthy?", "Threads may be blocked on storage, locks, network replies, or quotas. Idle CPU can coexist with a long request queue."),
  "how-the-cpu-executes-instructions": T(
    "The architectural model fetches instructions, decodes operations, reads operands, executes, and commits results. Modern CPUs pipeline and may execute out of order while preserving the required architectural behavior. Registers are fast working state; cache misses and unpredictable branches can stall useful progress even at a high clock rate.",
    "Compare summing a contiguous array with following a randomly linked list of equal length. Both are linear-time, but the list introduces dependent memory loads.",
    "Fetch|Load next instruction|Program counter;Execute|Wait for pointer operand|Cache miss stalls dependency;Retire|Commit completed instruction|Architectural state advances",
    "Big-O complexity does not capture cache locality, branch prediction, vectorization, or memory-level parallelism.",
    "Why can fewer instructions still take longer?", "Instructions can wait on data or dependencies. Measure elapsed time, cycles, and cache behavior instead of inferring speed from source length."),
  "privilege-levels": T(
    "Privilege modes restrict which instructions and memory mappings an application can access. A controlled syscall, trap, or interrupt enters a privileged handler; a validated return restores user execution. Crossing modes does not necessarily switch processes. The kernel must treat user addresses and lengths as untrusted inputs.",
    "A process asks to open a private file. Entering kernel mode gives the kernel authority to check access; it does not grant the caller that authority.",
    "User|Requests open|Unprivileged instruction stream;Kernel|Checks path and credentials|Access decision;User|Receives EACCES|Process identity unchanged",
    "Kernel mode is a CPU execution state; root is an operating-system identity. They are related but not interchangeable.",
    "Is every syscall a context switch?", "It is a privilege transition. A scheduler context switch occurs only if execution moves to another task, for example when the call blocks."),
  "the-memory-hierarchy": T(
    "Registers, CPU caches, RAM, and persistent storage trade capacity and cost against access latency. Caches exploit spatial and temporal locality. A dependent chain of misses limits throughput because later addresses are unknown until earlier loads complete. Exact latencies depend on hardware and workload, so use relative reasoning and measurements.",
    "Scan a row-major matrix by rows, then by columns. Both visit every element; contiguous row traversal usually uses each fetched cache line more effectively.",
    "Load|Fetch one cache line|Several adjacent elements;Row scan|Reuse neighboring bytes|Spatial locality;Column scan|Jump across rows|More misses possible",
    "A larger cache does not eliminate poor locality, conflict misses, or contention between cores.",
    "Why does batching often improve performance?", "It amortizes fixed overhead and can improve locality, but larger batches consume memory and may increase individual request latency."),
  "interrupts-exceptions-and-traps": T(
    "An interrupt normally originates outside the current instruction stream, such as device completion. An exception is associated with instruction execution, such as an invalid access. A deliberate trap can request a service or debugging action. Handlers save sufficient state, perform controlled work, and either resume or terminate the affected execution.",
    "Contrast a disk completion with a missing page during a load. Both enter kernel handling, but only the page fault is causally tied to that particular instruction.",
    "Load|Page absent|Synchronous exception;Kernel|Makes valid page available|May wait for I/O;Resume|Retry faulting instruction|Value becomes accessible",
    "A page fault is not automatically a bug. A protection violation and a recoverable demand fault have different outcomes.",
    "Why should interrupt handlers avoid long blocking work?", "They execute under special context constraints and can delay other work. Kernels defer suitable processing to contexts that may schedule or sleep."),
  "what-the-kernel-manages": T(
    "The kernel coordinates scheduling, address spaces, devices, files, networking, and access checks. Its objects connect abstractions: a task references an address space and descriptor table, and a descriptor references an open object. User-space libraries implement many conveniences without being part of the kernel.",
    "A web worker accepts a socket and writes a log. Identify the task, socket buffers, descriptor entries, cached file pages, and device requests involved.",
    "Worker|Owns descriptor table|Socket and log entries;Kernel|Tracks buffers and cached pages|Shared resource accounting;Device|Completes pending request|Waiting task may wake",
    "A library call may make zero, one, or many syscalls. Function names alone do not identify kernel work.",
    "Where should an application-specific retry policy live?", "Usually in user space where deadlines and business semantics are known. The kernel provides transport mechanisms, not application success criteria."),
  "kernel-architectures": T(
    "A monolithic design places many services in one privileged address space, enabling direct calls but enlarging the fault boundary. A microkernel moves selected services into isolated processes and communicates through IPC. Hybrid designs combine approaches. Performance and reliability depend on implementation and workload, not just the label.",
    "Compare a filesystem request implemented by an in-kernel function with one sent to a user-space service. Count boundary crossings and identify what happens if the service fails.",
    "Client|Requests file metadata|Common API;Boundary|Direct call or IPC message|Different isolation cost;Service|Returns metadata or failure|Recovery boundary matters",
    "A microkernel does not automatically make every service restartable, and loadable modules do not make a monolithic kernel a microkernel.",
    "What trade-off would you discuss for a driver?", "Isolation can contain driver faults, while extra communication and copying can add overhead. Evaluate both recovery requirements and measured hot-path cost."),
  "system-calls": T(
    "System calls expose controlled kernel operations through an ABI. Libraries wrap those interfaces and may translate errors or buffer work. Calls such as read and write return the amount actually transferred, which can be smaller than requested. Correct callers maintain progress, interpret errors, and respect object lifetimes.",
    "A write of 100 bytes returns 40. Advance the pointer by 40 and retain 60 bytes, instead of repeating the original buffer and duplicating output.",
    "Caller|Requests 100 bytes|Offset zero;Kernel|Accepts 40 bytes|Partial progress;Caller|Retries remaining 60|Offset forty",
    "A successful write is not necessarily durable storage or peer application processing. Each API has a specific success boundary.",
    "What state must a partial-write loop retain?", "The unsent offset, remaining length, deadline, and error state. On nonblocking descriptors it must wait for readiness rather than busy-loop."),
  "inside-a-system-call": T(
    "A syscall wrapper places arguments according to the architecture ABI and enters the kernel through a designated instruction. Entry code preserves state, dispatches the operation, validates user arguments, and returns a result. Blocking may involve wait queues and scheduling; a short completed syscall can return to the same task immediately.",
    "Trace read(fd, buffer, 8) when no socket bytes are available. Contrast a blocking descriptor with a nonblocking descriptor.",
    "Entry|Validate descriptor and buffer|Kernel execution;Wait|No bytes available|Sleep or return EAGAIN;Return|Bytes arrive or error reported|User code resumes",
    "Checking that a pointer is non-null is insufficient. The kernel must safely access the requested user range and handle faults.",
    "Where does syscall latency come from?", "Entry/exit overhead, validation, copying, contention, scheduling, and device or network waiting. Separate CPU time from elapsed waiting time."),
  "boot-elf-and-the-abi": T(
    "Firmware and a boot loader prepare hardware and load the kernel; the kernel initializes subsystems and starts user-space initialization. An ELF executable describes loadable segments and entry information on many Unix systems. An ABI defines binary conventions such as calling conventions, register usage, alignment, and syscall numbers.",
    "A binary compiled for a different CPU fails before main. Distinguish incompatible machine instructions from a missing dynamic loader or shared library.",
    "Boot|Kernel initializes platform|Starts user space;Loader|Checks executable format|Maps loadable segments;Runtime|Resolves dependencies|Transfers control to program",
    "Source compatibility is not binary compatibility. A matching language does not guarantee a matching architecture, ABI, or loader.",
    "Why can a file exist yet execution report a missing file?", "The executable can name an interpreter or dynamic loader that is absent. Inspect the binary format and interpreter path before assuming the executable disappeared."),
  "linux-windows-and-macos": T(
    "All three systems schedule tasks, protect memory, and expose I/O, but their public APIs and internal implementations differ. Linux commonly exposes file descriptors and epoll; Windows uses handles and completion-oriented facilities such as IOCP; macOS provides POSIX interfaces and kqueue. Portable libraries normalize only part of these differences.",
    "Port a concurrent server by separating protocol parsing from the platform event loop. Preserve partial-I/O handling and shutdown semantics while replacing the notification backend.",
    "Protocol|Parses framed messages|Portable state machine;Adapter|Uses epoll kqueue or IOCP|Platform-specific I/O;Tests|Exercise EOF and cancellation|Same application contract",
    "Do not assume filesystem case behavior, path syntax, process creation, or signal semantics match across platforms.",
    "What should a portability test focus on?", "Observable guarantees: byte ordering, resource cleanup, error handling, deadlines, and shutdown. Avoid tests that assume identical kernel event sequences."),
  "program-vs-process": T(
    "A program is code and associated data stored in an executable form. A process is a running instance with identity, credentials, address mappings, and resources. Multiple processes may execute the same program while maintaining independent mutable state, even when read-only executable pages are physically shared.",
    "Start the same command twice. Each instance has a distinct PID and stack, but the kernel may share physical pages containing the executable's unchanged instructions.",
    "Program|One executable file|Persistent instructions;Process A|PID and private state|First instance;Process B|Different PID and stack|Same code can be shared",
    "Identical virtual addresses in two processes do not imply identical physical memory or permission to access each other.",
    "Does editing the executable change an already running stack?", "No. The process has runtime state and mappings independent of a source edit; replacing binaries and modifying mapped files have platform-specific behavior."),
  "the-process-control-block": T(
    "A process control block is the conceptual kernel record needed to manage a process. It includes or references identity, scheduling state, saved execution context, credentials, memory mappings, and open resources. Real kernels distribute this information across structures, and threads can share some referenced objects while retaining separate scheduling state.",
    "Pause a running task and later resume it. Identify the saved instruction pointer and registers versus shared file and memory objects that need not be copied.",
    "Running|Registers live on CPU|Task selected;Deschedule|Save execution context|Task record updated;Resume|Restore saved context|Continues from prior point",
    "A PCB is not a full duplicate of the process's memory. References and shared structures are essential to efficient management.",
    "Why must each thread have its own saved stack pointer?", "Each thread has an independent call stack and execution position, even when it shares an address space with other threads."),
  "process-states": T(
    "A runnable task can execute when scheduled, a running task currently owns a CPU, and a blocked task awaits an event. Completion changes a blocked task to runnable; it does not guarantee immediate execution. Preemption returns a running task to the runnable set. Exact state names vary across kernels.",
    "A server reads an empty socket, waits, and wakes when a packet arrives while another task is still using the CPU.",
    "Running|read finds no data|Task blocks;Blocked|Packet arrives|Task becomes runnable;Runnable|Scheduler selects task|Read can complete",
    "Waking a task and scheduling it are different events. Queueing delay can remain after the awaited event has completed.",
    "Can adding CPU cores fix a blocked disk read?", "Not directly. It can reduce runnable delay, but the missing data still depends on storage completion or a changed I/O strategy."),
  "fork-exec-wait-exit": T(
    "fork creates a child with a separate process identity and initially related state; exec replaces the calling process image; exit records termination; wait collects a child's status. Descriptor inheritance and copy-on-write mappings make creation efficient, but applications must intentionally close unused descriptors and reap children.",
    "A shell forks, the child execs a command, and the parent waits. The child keeps its PID across exec and reports status 0 after successful completion.",
    "fork|Parent and child return differently|Child gets new PID;exec|Child image replaced|PID retained;wait|Parent collects exit status|Zombie record released",
    "After fork in a multithreaded process, the child inherits only the calling thread; inherited lock state restricts safe work before exec.",
    "Why close the unused end of a pipe after fork?", "An inherited write end keeps the pipe logically open, so a reader may never observe EOF even after the intended writer exits."),
  "clone-and-what-fork-shares": T(
    "Linux clone-style creation selects which resources a new task shares, including address space, descriptor table, filesystem context, and signal-related state under valid flag combinations. Threads and fork-like processes use related kernel mechanisms with different sharing choices. Shared state requires explicit synchronization and lifetime management.",
    "Compare a task sharing an address space with a fork-like child using copy-on-write. A store is visible to the former's peers but ordinarily private to the latter.",
    "Create|Choose sharing flags|Kernel creates task;Store|Modify one mapped variable|Visibility follows sharing;Observe|Peer sees shared update|Separate address space stays private",
    "clone flags are constrained by kernel rules. Do not treat arbitrary combinations as valid or use raw clone as a portable thread library.",
    "Why can threads share files but have independent registers?", "Resource sharing and execution context are separate dimensions. Each schedulable task still needs its own saved CPU state."),
  "zombies-and-orphans": T(
    "A zombie has terminated but retains a small status record until its parent reaps it. An orphan is a process whose parent has exited; it can continue running and is reparented to an appropriate reaper. These describe different lifecycle properties, and an orphan is not necessarily a zombie.",
    "A parent launches 100 short commands but never waits. The commands release normal execution resources, yet their unreaped status records accumulate.",
    "Child|Exits with status 7|Execution ends;Parent|Has not called wait|Zombie status retained;Reap|wait collects status|Kernel record released",
    "Sending another termination signal cannot kill an already dead zombie. Repair the parent's reaping behavior.",
    "Why does a container init process matter?", "It should forward relevant signals and reap adopted children. Without proper lifecycle handling, shutdown and child cleanup can fail."),
  "context-switching": T(
    "A context switch saves one task's execution state and restores another's. Switching address spaces can add translation and cache effects, while switching threads in one process can retain more shared context. Direct register work is only part of the cost; disrupted locality can dominate afterward.",
    "Two CPU-bound tasks alternate on one core. Track saved registers at each switch and explain why their combined useful work is less than an ideal zero-overhead schedule.",
    "Task A|Runs for one quantum|Registers change;Scheduler|Saves A and restores B|Useful work pauses;Task B|Resumes execution|Caches may be colder",
    "A mode switch can happen without a task switch, and a high switch count alone does not prove a performance problem.",
    "When is switching beneficial despite overhead?", "When another runnable task can make progress while the current task waits, or when preemption is needed for fairness and responsiveness."),
  "signals": T(
    "Signals notify a process or thread of asynchronous events. Dispositions can perform a default action, ignore eligible signals, or run a handler. Masks defer delivery. Handlers interrupt ordinary execution, so only async-signal-safe operations belong there; a common design records a flag or writes to a safe notification channel.",
    "A service receives SIGTERM and asks its main loop to stop accepting work, finish bounded in-flight requests, and exit cleanly.",
    "Delivery|SIGTERM arrives|Handler context;Notify|Set termination flag|Minimal safe work;Main loop|Drains and closes resources|Ordinary cleanup context",
    "Calling arbitrary allocation, logging, or lock-taking code from a handler can deadlock. SIGKILL cannot be handled.",
    "Why might a blocking syscall return EINTR?", "Signal delivery can interrupt the wait. The caller must follow that syscall's restart rules and preserve partial progress where applicable."),
  "process-groups-sessions-and-jobs": T(
    "A process group collects related processes for job control, and a session groups process groups with terminal relationships. A shell pipeline commonly belongs to one process group so terminal signals can affect the entire foreground job. A job is a shell-level abstraction rather than a separate kernel execution entity.",
    "A shell runs a producer piped into a consumer. Ctrl-C targets the foreground process group, allowing both sides of the pipeline to stop.",
    "Shell|Creates pipeline group|Two related processes;Terminal|Sends interrupt to foreground group|Both receive signal;Shell|Collects child statuses|Prompt regains control",
    "Killing only a parent's PID need not stop its children. Group membership and service supervision must be designed explicitly.",
    "Why does a daemon detach from a terminal?", "It should not depend on an interactive terminal's foreground job control or session lifetime; modern service managers provide a clearer supervision model."),
  "why-scheduling-exists": T(
    "When runnable tasks outnumber available CPUs, a scheduler chooses who runs next. Goals include responsiveness, throughput, fairness, and deadline satisfaction; these can conflict. Preemption prevents one ordinary task from holding a CPU indefinitely, while blocking naturally releases it until the awaited event occurs.",
    "A short interactive request arrives behind a long batch job on one core. Compare waiting for batch completion with allowing a preemption point.",
    "Batch|Occupies the CPU|Interactive task queues;Preempt|Save batch context|Scheduler chooses next;Interactive|Completes short work|Better response time",
    "Maximizing utilization alone can produce poor tail latency. Scheduling policy must reflect workload objectives.",
    "Why not give every task a tiny quantum?", "Responsiveness improves only until switching and locality costs dominate. Measure useful work and response-time distributions together."),
  "scheduling-metrics": T(
    "Turnaround is completion minus arrival; response is first execution minus arrival; waiting is time runnable but not executing. For simple CPU-only examples, waiting equals turnaround minus CPU service. With I/O, blocked time must be separated. Throughput counts completed jobs per time interval, while utilization measures busy capacity.",
    "A CPU-only job arrives at 2 ms, first runs at 5 ms, needs 4 ms of CPU, and finishes at 12 ms. Response is 3, turnaround 10, and waiting 6 ms.",
    "Arrival|Time 2|Ready for CPU;First run|Time 5|Response 3 ms;Completion|Time 12|Turnaround 10 and wait 6 ms",
    "Do not subtract only CPU time to infer runnable waiting when the task also blocks on I/O.",
    "Can average response improve while one job starves?", "Yes. Aggregate averages can hide extreme waits; report tail latency and fairness alongside throughput."),
  "fcfs-sjf-and-srtf": T(
    "FCFS runs jobs in arrival order without preemption. SJF chooses the shortest available job when the CPU becomes free; SRTF preempts when a newly available job has less remaining work. Short-job policies can reduce mean waiting under their assumptions but need burst estimates and can disadvantage long jobs.",
    "A arrives at 0 with burst 8; B arrives at 1 with burst 2. FCFS completes A at 8 and B at 10. SRTF runs A 0–1, B 1–3, A 3–10.",
    "t=0|A starts with 8|Only ready job;t=1|B arrives with 2|SRTF preempts A;t=3|B complete and A resumes|A finishes at 10",
    "SJF is not clairvoyant in production: burst estimates can be wrong, and starvation prevention still matters.",
    "Does nonpreemptive SJF interrupt A when B arrives?", "No. It makes its next choice only when A stops or completes. That is the key difference from SRTF."),
  "round-robin": T(
    "Round robin gives each runnable task a bounded time quantum and places unfinished tasks at the queue tail. It offers repeated opportunities to run without requiring burst predictions. Smaller quanta improve first-response opportunities but increase switching costs; a quantum larger than all bursts approaches FCFS behavior.",
    "At time zero A needs 5 units and B needs 3. With quantum 2 and no overhead, the sequence is A2, B2, A2, B1, A1; B finishes at 7 and A at 8.",
    "0–2|A runs two units|A remaining 3;2–4|B runs two units|B remaining 1;4–8|A2 then B1 then A1|Both complete",
    "State arrival order, quantum, and context-switch cost before calculating a schedule. Different assumptions produce different answers.",
    "Why can turnaround worsen even as responsiveness improves?", "Interleaving delays completion of some jobs and adds overhead, although each task gets an earlier opportunity to run."),
  "priority-scheduling-and-aging": T(
    "Priority scheduling favors tasks according to a ranking, either preemptively or at dispatch points. A continuous supply of higher-priority work can starve lower-priority jobs. Aging raises waiting tasks' effective priority over time, trading strict preference for eventual service. Priority numbering conventions vary by API.",
    "A maintenance job waits while urgent requests keep arriving. Increase its effective priority after each waiting interval until it receives a slice.",
    "Queue|Maintenance has low rank|Urgent jobs selected;Age|Waiting time accumulates|Effective rank improves;Dispatch|Maintenance becomes eligible|Starvation bounded by policy",
    "Aging addresses starvation; it does not by itself solve priority inversion caused by a lock owner.",
    "How would you keep urgent work responsive while aging?", "Bound maintenance service, choose a controlled aging rate, and measure deadline misses rather than simply making every task highest priority."),
  "multilevel-queues-and-mlfq": T(
    "Multilevel queues divide work into classes with separate scheduling rules. A multilevel feedback queue additionally moves tasks between levels based on observed behavior. Short interactive bursts can stay near the top; CPU-heavy work is demoted. Periodic boosts and accounting across voluntary yields prevent starvation and simple gaming.",
    "A task consumes its entire high-priority allotment and moves down. A periodic boost later gives it another opportunity while a short interactive task responds quickly.",
    "Top queue|Short quantum|New task starts;Consume allotment|CPU-bound task demoted|Longer lower-level slice;Boost|Waiting task promoted|Avoid permanent starvation",
    "Resetting the allotment on every voluntary yield lets a task game the policy. Track cumulative service within the level.",
    "Why not permanently label tasks interactive or batch?", "Behavior changes over time. Feedback adapts to observed CPU use, though it still needs carefully chosen fairness rules."),
  "multiprocessor-scheduling": T(
    "Multiple CPUs allow parallel execution but add load balancing, affinity, cache sharing, and NUMA placement decisions. Per-CPU queues reduce central contention while stealing or migration redistributes work. Keeping a task near its data improves locality, but excessive affinity can leave some CPUs idle while others are overloaded.",
    "CPU 0 has four runnable tasks and CPU 1 is idle. Move work only after considering whether its memory resides near CPU 0 and whether the imbalance is temporary.",
    "Observe|CPU 0 overloaded|CPU 1 idle;Balance|Migrate one runnable task|More parallel capacity;Measure|Compare runtime and memory stalls|Locality may worsen",
    "Four threads do not imply fourfold speedup. Shared locks, memory bandwidth, and serial work can dominate.",
    "When would pinning help?", "For stable latency or locality-sensitive workloads with known placement. It can hurt under changing load, so compare against unpinned measurements."),
  "real-time-scheduling": T(
    "Real-time systems care about completion before deadlines, not merely average speed. Rate-monotonic policies prioritize shorter periods under a periodic task model; earliest-deadline-first chooses the nearest deadline. Schedulability reasoning requires execution bounds, periods, deadlines, preemption assumptions, and overhead. Admission control protects existing commitments.",
    "Two ideal periodic tasks require 1 unit every 4 and 2 units every 5. Utilization is 0.25 + 0.40 = 0.65; assess against the chosen scheduler's assumptions.",
    "Declare|C1=1 T1=4 and C2=2 T2=5|Known task model;Compute|Utilization 0.65|Capacity estimate;Validate|Include blocking and overhead|Deadline guarantee needs bounds",
    "Low average utilization does not guarantee deadlines when execution time, lock blocking, or interrupt delays are unbounded.",
    "Does real-time mean faster?", "It means timing requirements are part of correctness. A slower but predictably bounded system can satisfy a deadline better than a faster unpredictable one."),
  "linux-scheduling-classes": T(
    "Linux exposes different scheduling policies for ordinary fair work, fixed-priority real-time work, and deadline reservations. SCHED_FIFO and SCHED_RR have different equal-priority rotation behavior; SCHED_DEADLINE requires runtime and timing parameters. Permissions, admission checks, and resource controls constrain their use. Class behavior is kernel-version dependent.",
    "An ordinary web worker competes with a privileged real-time loop. Explain why increasing worker threads may not recover CPU service while the loop remains runnable.",
    "Fair task|Runnable request worker|Normal scheduling class;RT task|Higher class work runs|Fair task can wait;Control|Bound privileged workload|Restore predictable service",
    "Assigning real-time priority to a buggy loop can harm the whole machine. Priority is not a general throughput optimization.",
    "How do FIFO and RR differ at equal RT priority?", "FIFO generally runs until blocking, yielding, or preemption by higher priority; RR adds time slicing among runnable tasks of equal priority."),
  "fair-scheduling-cfs-to-eevdf": T(
    "CFS-style reasoning tracks weighted virtual runtime to approximate fair CPU sharing. EEVDF additionally distinguishes eligibility through service lag and selects an eligible task with an early virtual deadline. This separates fair entitlement from latency preference. These are virtual service quantities, not application wall-clock deadline guarantees.",
    "Task A is owed CPU service and task B has already received more than its share. An early virtual deadline alone should not allow B to keep taking service while ineligible.",
    "Account|Compare service against entitlement|Compute lag;Filter|Keep eligible tasks|Exclude overserved candidates;Choose|Earliest eligible virtual deadline|Run and update accounting",
    "Do not present ordinary fair scheduling as hard real-time scheduling. Details evolve with kernel versions.",
    "Why separate eligibility from deadline ordering?", "Eligibility protects fair entitlement, while deadline ordering lets eligible short requests receive more responsive service."),
  "what-a-thread-is-to-the-kernel": T("A kernel thread is an independently schedulable execution context with registers, stack, and scheduling state. Threads belonging to one process normally share its address space and open resources. Shared memory makes communication cheap but means a faulty write can corrupt another thread's data.", "Two request threads share a cache but execute separate call stacks. One blocks on a socket while the other remains runnable.", "Thread A|Waits for input|Blocked;Thread B|Runs cache lookup|Same address space;Scheduler|Wakes A after input|Separate execution state", "Shared address space does not make compound updates atomic.", "What is private to a thread?", "Its execution registers, stack, and thread-local storage are conceptually private; ordinary heap objects may be shared."),
  "processes-vs-threads": T("Processes provide separate address spaces and a stronger fault boundary, while threads share memory and resources within a process. Processes usually communicate through explicit IPC; threads can communicate through shared objects with synchronization. Both can run concurrently or in parallel, depending on available CPUs.", "Compare running image decoders as threads versus worker processes. A memory corruption bug in a thread can damage the entire service; a process boundary can contain it.", "Dispatch|Choose worker boundary|Thread or process;Decode|Worker handles image|Shared or separate memory;Failure|Worker crashes|Recovery scope differs", "Processes still share external resources and can contend on the same database or files.", "When is a process pool worth its overhead?", "When isolation, independent restart, or runtime constraints outweigh IPC and memory costs."),
  "user-and-kernel-threads": T("User-level schedulers can multiplex many logical tasks onto fewer kernel threads. Kernel threads are visible to the OS scheduler and can execute on separate CPUs. In a many-to-one design, a blocking system call can stop every user task on that kernel thread unless the runtime arranges nonblocking I/O or offloading.", "A runtime has 1,000 coroutines on two kernel threads. A coroutine that performs an uncooperative blocking call occupies one of those two carriers.", "Runtime|Queues 1000 logical tasks|Two kernel carriers;Block|One carrier waits in syscall|Capacity reduced;Adapt|Use async I/O or offload|Other tasks can progress", "A thousand logical tasks do not imply a thousand-way CPU parallelism.", "Why are lightweight tasks cheaper?", "They can use smaller or growable stacks and runtime scheduling, while still depending on kernel resources for actual execution and I/O."),
  "thread-creation-and-teardown": T("Thread creation allocates execution state and a stack, then makes the task schedulable. Joining waits for termination and collects the associated resources; detaching chooses automatic cleanup instead of later joining. Shared objects must outlive every thread that accesses them, and cancellation requires a defined cleanup protocol.", "A request launches a thread using a pointer to a local variable, then returns. The worker may access storage whose lifetime has ended.", "Create|Pass work to thread|Ownership must transfer;Execute|Worker reads shared input|Input must remain alive;Join|Wait before releasing input|Safe lifetime boundary", "Sleeping briefly is not a substitute for joining or transferring ownership.", "Why use cooperative cancellation?", "It lets the worker stop at a known point and release locks and resources consistently."),
  "what-a-context-switch-costs": T("Thread switches include saving and restoring registers plus indirect cache, branch-prediction, and translation effects. Switching within one address space may avoid some address-space changes but still disrupt working sets. Costs depend on CPU architecture, kernel behavior, and workload, so a single universal nanosecond figure is misleading.", "Compare two threads updating one small array with two workers scanning large disjoint arrays. Equal switch counts can yield very different cache miss costs.", "Before|Working set warm|Fast repeated access;Switch|Different task runs|Caches compete;After|Original task resumes|May refill working set", "Context-switch counts need correlation with CPU time and latency before tuning.", "How would you measure the useful effect?", "Hold workload constant, vary scheduling pressure, and compare elapsed time, cycles, cache misses, and tail latency."),
  "thread-pools-and-sizing": T("A pool amortizes thread creation and bounds active concurrency. CPU-heavy work often starts near available CPU capacity; blocking workloads may need more threads, subject to memory, downstream limits, and queueing. A rough estimate is cores times one plus wait-to-compute ratio, but measurement and admission control must validate it.", "Four cores and tasks averaging 2 ms compute plus 6 ms waiting suggest 4 × (1 + 6/2) = 16 workers as an initial experiment, not a guarantee.", "Estimate|Four cores and ratio three|Start near 16;Bound|Limit queue and downstream calls|Avoid overload;Measure|Observe throughput and p99|Adjust from evidence", "An unbounded queue hides overload until latency and memory explode.", "Why can more workers reduce throughput?", "They can increase contention, switching, memory pressure, and downstream saturation while adding no CPU capacity."),
  "runtime-threading-models": T("Language execution units do not map uniformly to OS threads. Go schedules goroutines over kernel threads; Java supports platform threads and lightweight virtual threads; Python execution behavior depends on interpreter build and workload. Async tasks usually cooperate on an event loop. Blocking and CPU parallelism must be analyzed at each layer.", "A CPU-heavy loop inside an async handler monopolizes its event-loop thread even when many other handlers are awaiting network data.", "Async loop|Many tasks registered|One execution thread;CPU loop|No suspension point|Other tasks delayed;Offload|Use appropriate worker execution|Loop becomes responsive", "The keyword async does not make CPU-bound work parallel or automatically nonblocking.", "What should you ask before sizing workers?", "Identify logical tasks, kernel threads, runtime locking, CPU limits, blocking behavior, and the actual bottleneck."),
  "race-conditions-and-atomicity": T("A race occurs when correctness depends on an uncontrolled ordering of concurrent operations. Incrementing a variable commonly involves load, add, and store; atomicity of individual loads does not make the sequence atomic. Atomic read-modify-write instructions or locks can preserve the invariant, but multiple related fields may require a larger critical section.", "Two threads read counter 0, each computes 1, and both store 1. Two completed increments produce only one recorded increment.", "Read|A=0 and B=0|Same initial counter;Compute|Both calculate 1|Independent registers;Store|Both write 1|Lost update", "volatile is not a portable replacement for atomic operations or synchronization.", "Is an atomic balance enough for a transfer?", "Not alone: debiting one account and crediting another form a cross-object invariant requiring an appropriate transaction or locking protocol."),
  "memory-ordering-and-false-sharing": T("Memory ordering defines which writes another thread can observe after synchronization. Release/acquire operations can publish data through an agreed synchronization variable; relaxed atomicity alone does not establish that publication relationship. False sharing occurs when independent variables occupy one coherence line and different cores repeatedly invalidate each other's cached copies.", "A producer writes payload then publishes ready with release; a consumer acquires ready before reading payload. Separately, pad hot independent counters to investigate coherence traffic.", "Producer|Write payload then release ready|Publication order;Consumer|Acquire observes ready|Payload becomes visible;Counters|Separate contended cache lines|Less false sharing possible", "Padding fixes neither data races nor every cache problem; it increases memory footprint.", "Are atomic operations always sequentially consistent?", "No. Memory-order choices provide different guarantees. Explain the required happens-before relation before weakening ordering."),
  "mutexes-semaphores-and-condition-variables": T("A mutex protects exclusive access, a semaphore counts available permits, and a condition variable lets threads wait for a predicate guarded by a mutex. Waiting releases the mutex atomically with entering the wait protocol, then reacquires it before returning. Always recheck the predicate in a loop because wakeups do not reserve the condition.", "A consumer waits while a queue is empty. A producer adds one item under the mutex and signals; competing consumers must still check whether an item remains.", "Consumer|Queue empty under lock|Wait releases mutex;Producer|Enqueue item and signal|Predicate changes;Consumer|Reacquire and recheck|Consume only if nonempty", "A condition variable is not a stored event counter. Signals can occur before a waiter exists.", "Why use while instead of if?", "Spurious wakeups and competing consumers can leave the predicate false when the waiter finally reacquires the lock."),
  "how-blocking-works": T("Blocking places a task on a wait structure and removes it from runnable competition until an event makes progress possible. Linux futexes combine a user-space value with kernel assistance for contention: uncontended lock operations can remain in user space, while a checked wait avoids sleeping after the relevant value has already changed.", "A thread fails to acquire a lock. Before sleeping, it checks the expected lock value through the futex protocol so an intervening unlock is not lost.", "Fast path|Try atomic acquire|No syscall if successful;Contended|Wait only if value matches|Avoid missed transition;Wake|Owner releases and notifies|Waiter retries acquisition", "Wakeup is not ownership transfer; the awakened thread must retry the actual synchronization operation.", "Why combine user and kernel mechanisms?", "The common uncontended path is cheap, while the kernel can park contended threads without wasting CPU spinning."),
  "spinlocks-and-interrupt-context": T("A spinlock waits by repeatedly checking ownership rather than sleeping. It is appropriate only for short protected work under suitable execution constraints. Kernel interrupt context often cannot sleep; synchronization may need to prevent local interrupt reentry as well as cross-CPU access. Lock type and context rules are platform-specific.", "A CPU holds a lock when a local interrupt handler tries to acquire the same lock. Without the required interrupt discipline, the handler spins while the owner cannot resume.", "Task|Owns shared spinlock|Critical section;Interrupt|Attempts same lock|Owner suspended;Deadlock|Handler waits indefinitely|Need correct context discipline", "Never perform arbitrary blocking I/O while holding a lock that must not sleep.", "When is a mutex preferable?", "When waits can be long and sleeping is permitted, parking avoids consuming CPU while the owner performs its work."),
  "deadlock": T("Deadlock requires a cycle of tasks that cannot progress under the current resource ownership. The classic conditions are mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no forced preemption, and circular wait. A global lock order prevents cycles; detection finds cycles after they form; avoidance requires assumptions about future resource needs.", "Thread A holds L1 and waits for L2 while B holds L2 and waits for L1. Require both to acquire L1 before L2.", "A|Owns L1 and needs L2|Waiting edge to B;B|Owns L2 and needs L1|Cycle completed;Order|Acquire L1 before L2|Circular wait prevented", "Timeouts require rollback and cleanup; merely timing out without releasing resources does not restore correctness.", "How is deadlock different from slow contention?", "Contention may resolve when an owner progresses. A deadlock cycle cannot resolve through ordinary execution of the waiting participants."),
  "starvation-livelock-and-priority-inversion": T("Starvation means a task repeatedly loses access despite system progress. Livelock means tasks keep changing state without completing useful work. Priority inversion occurs when high-priority work waits on a lower-priority owner that intermediate work delays. Priority inheritance temporarily raises the owner's scheduling priority to help release the dependency.", "High-priority H waits for a lock held by L. Medium-priority M preempts L; inheritance lets L run long enough to unlock for H.", "L|Holds lock|H becomes blocked;M|Preempts L|Inversion prolonged;Inherit|L receives priority boost|Unlock enables H", "Random backoff may help livelock but is not a general solution to unfair scheduling or cyclic locking.", "Does priority inheritance eliminate all deadlocks?", "No. It addresses scheduling inversion, not arbitrary resource cycles or incorrect lock ordering."),
  "pipes-and-fifos": T("A pipe is a kernel-buffered byte stream with read and write ends. A FIFO adds a filesystem name for connecting processes. Reads may return partial data; EOF requires all write references to close. Small-write atomicity rules are distinct from message framing, and a full pipe creates backpressure.", "A parent and child inherit both ends of a pipe. The reader cannot see EOF until every unused inherited write end is closed.", "Create|Two descriptors|Read and write ends;Transfer|Writer sends bytes|Reader may read chunks;Close|Last writer closes|Reader eventually gets EOF", "Do not assume one write equals one read or that a pipe buffer is unlimited.", "What happens when the reader disappears?", "A write can fail with a broken-pipe condition and may deliver SIGPIPE on Unix, depending on handling and API options."),
  "shared-memory": T("Shared memory maps the same backing storage into multiple address spaces. It avoids copying every message through a stream, but does not supply synchronization, ownership, or recovery automatically. Use process-shared synchronization or an explicitly designed atomic protocol, and store offsets rather than process-specific pointers when mappings may differ.", "Two processes share a ring buffer. The producer writes a slot before publishing its index; the consumer observes publication before reading the slot.", "Producer|Fill slot 3|Not yet published;Publish|Advance index with ordering|Consumer may observe;Consumer|Read then release slot|Space becomes reusable", "A pointer valid in one process may refer to unrelated memory in another mapping.", "Why can shared memory be harder than a socket?", "The application owns consistency, buffer reuse, crash recovery, and synchronization instead of relying on a kernel-managed byte stream."),
  "message-queues-and-signals": T("Message queues preserve message boundaries and can decouple senders from receivers within bounded capacity. Signals are lightweight notifications with limited payload and platform-specific queueing behavior; ordinary signals may coalesce. Neither mechanism removes the need for overload handling, ownership rules, and a policy for receiver failure.", "Ten identical ordinary notifications arrive while blocked. Do not assume ten handler executions; use a queue or counter when each event must be represented.", "Sender|Produces ten events|Need exact accounting;Signal|Notifications may coalesce|Wakeup only;Queue|Store explicit event records|Bound capacity and retry policy", "A signal should not be treated as a reliable unbounded work queue.", "When is a notification enough?", "When the receiver can inspect authoritative state after waking, such as draining an existing queue until empty."),
  "unix-domain-sockets": T("Unix domain sockets communicate locally through kernel socket abstractions without IP routing. They support stream or datagram semantics and can expose peer credentials or descriptor passing on supporting systems. Filesystem names have permission and lifecycle implications; local transport still needs framing, authentication decisions, and backpressure.", "A supervisor opens a file and passes its descriptor to a worker over a Unix socket. The receiver obtains a usable reference, not the sender's integer descriptor value.", "Supervisor|Owns open file|Descriptor in local table;Send|Attach descriptor via ancillary data|Kernel transfers reference;Worker|Receives new descriptor number|Same underlying open object", "A socket path's existence does not prove the service is alive or that every caller is authorized.", "Why pass a descriptor instead of a filename?", "It transfers an already opened capability and avoids requiring the worker to resolve and reopen the path with broader privileges."),
  "network-sockets-and-the-kernel": T("The kernel tracks listening sockets, connection establishment, accepted sockets, and receive/send buffers. A listen backlog relates to pending connection handling, not the application's total processing capacity. Accepting a connection produces a separate connected socket; slow application reads or writes propagate pressure through buffers and transport flow control.", "An accept loop pauses during a long computation. New connections accumulate in kernel queues even though established workers are otherwise healthy.", "Listen|Kernel receives connection attempts|Pending work;Accept|Application drains completed connections|New connected descriptor;Process|Worker handles bytes|Separate capacity limit", "Increasing backlog can absorb a burst but cannot repair a permanently insufficient accept or processing rate.", "Why monitor queue depth and accept latency?", "They distinguish connection admission delays from later application processing and indicate whether the accept loop is keeping up."),
  "zero-copy-transfers": T("Zero-copy APIs reduce selected data copies between user and kernel buffers; they do not mean data never moves through memory or devices. sendfile can transfer file data to a socket, splice connects supported kernel buffers, and mmap exposes file-backed pages. Lifetimes, supported endpoints, and encryption paths affect the benefits.", "Serve a static file using a read/write loop versus sendfile. The latter can avoid the application's intermediate copy while the network still transmits every byte.", "File|Pages available in cache|Source data;Kernel|Transfers through supported path|Avoid user intermediate buffer;Socket|Queues data for transmission|Backpressure still applies", "mmap still incurs page faults and memory access; calling it zero-cost hides real work.", "When might zero-copy not improve latency?", "For small transfers dominated by setup or when transformation, compression, encryption, or unsupported endpoints require other processing."),
  "the-io-path": T("A buffered file write typically copies bytes into kernel-managed cached pages, marks them dirty, and returns before durable device completion. Filesystem mapping and the block layer later submit requests through a driver. Completion and persistence are separate milestones, and errors can surface after the initial write.", "A log append returns successfully, then power fails before write-back. Explain why the latest record may be absent without a durability protocol.", "write|Bytes enter page cache|Dirty memory;Write-back|Filesystem submits device request|I/O in progress;Persist|Required flush completes|Durability boundary reached", "A successful close is not a substitute for checking the required fsync and directory persistence guarantees.", "Why does the OS buffer file writes?", "Buffering can combine operations and avoid immediate device waits, at the cost of memory usage and a delayed persistence boundary."),
  "device-controllers-interrupts-and-dma": T("A driver programs a device controller, which manages hardware operations. DMA allows a device to transfer data to or from memory without the CPU copying each byte. Interrupts or polling report progress. Buffer mapping, cache coherence, ordering, and ownership must remain valid until the device finishes using the memory.", "A network card DMA-writes an incoming packet into a prepared buffer, then signals completion so software can process it.", "Driver|Posts receive buffer|Device owns buffer;DMA|NIC writes packet bytes|CPU not copying each byte;Completion|Driver observes result|Software may consume buffer", "DMA reduces CPU copy work but still consumes memory bandwidth and requires careful synchronization.", "Why cannot a DMA buffer be freed immediately?", "The device may still access it. Reusing the memory before completion can corrupt unrelated data."),
  "blocking-and-non-blocking-io": T("A blocking operation may park the caller until progress is possible. A nonblocking operation returns promptly when it would otherwise wait, commonly with EAGAIN or EWOULDBLOCK on Unix. Both may transfer fewer bytes than requested. Nonblocking mode requires retaining unfinished state and integrating with readiness notification.", "A nonblocking socket has no bytes available. Register interest and resume after readiness instead of repeatedly calling read in a tight loop.", "Read|No data available|EAGAIN;Wait|Register read readiness|Other work runs;Resume|Read available bytes|Preserve framing state", "Nonblocking does not mean the entire program is asynchronous or that every operation has zero CPU cost.", "How is EOF different from EAGAIN?", "EOF indicates the stream's receive direction has ended; EAGAIN means no progress is available now and future data may arrive."),
  "synchronous-and-asynchronous-io": T("Synchronous I/O returns the operation's result through the call, while asynchronous I/O separates submission from completion. Blocking versus nonblocking describes waiting behavior and is a related but distinct axis. An async caller must retain buffers and request identity until completion, cancellation, or another documented terminal state.", "Submit reads for two independent blocks. The second can complete first, so attach request IDs and place results by identity rather than submission order.", "Submit|Requests A and B|Buffers retained;Complete|B finishes first|Match request ID;Finish|A completes later|Release each buffer safely", "Cancellation requests do not universally guarantee immediate cancellation or immediate buffer reuse.", "What state does async I/O add?", "In-flight request identity, ownership, completion status, error handling, and coordination with shutdown."),
  "io-multiplexing": T("select and poll inspect sets of descriptors for readiness; epoll maintains an interest set and returns ready events on Linux. Readiness means an operation is likely to make progress without blocking, not that a complete application message exists. Applications still need nonblocking I/O, partial-write state, and descriptor lifecycle discipline.", "A server monitors 10,000 mostly idle sockets. Only three become readable, so process those events and preserve incomplete messages for later.", "Register|Track socket interests|Mostly idle set;Ready|Three sockets reported|Work subset;Drain|Read until appropriate stopping point|Keep partial messages", "A readiness event can race with another consumer; handle EAGAIN even after notification.", "Why remove write interest when no output is queued?", "Sockets are often writable continuously, so unnecessary write notifications can create a busy event loop."),
  "readiness-and-completion-models": T("Readiness reports that an operation may progress; completion reports the result of an operation already submitted. Level-triggered readiness persists while the condition holds, whereas edge-triggered notification emphasizes transitions. An edge-triggered consumer normally drains a nonblocking descriptor until EAGAIN to avoid leaving unread data without a new transition.", "Ten bytes arrive, but an edge-triggered handler reads only four. Six remain; waiting blindly for another edge can stall processing.", "Arrival|Ten bytes buffered|Readiness transition;Partial read|Four consumed|Six remain;Drain|Read until EAGAIN|Ready for next transition", "Completion events still require checking byte counts and errors; completed does not mean successful or full-length.", "Why is level triggering often simpler?", "Remaining readiness continues to be reported, reducing the risk of missing work after a partial drain, though fairness still needs attention."),
  "io-uring": T("io_uring is a Linux submission/completion interface using shared rings. The application submits operation descriptions and later consumes result records associated with request identifiers. Independent requests can finish out of order; buffer lifetimes and explicit dependencies matter. Supported operations and security restrictions depend on the running kernel and configuration.", "Submit reads A and B with distinct user_data values. If B completes first, inspect its result and release only B's completed buffer.", "SQ|Enqueue A and B|Unique request identifiers;Kernel|Processes operations|Order not assumed;CQ|Receive B result then A|Match and check each result", "Do not assume all operations or modes have identical completion counts; multishot and special flags change the contract.", "Why is one outstanding stream receive simpler?", "It preserves a clear association between stream order and buffers; overlapping receives require explicit ordering guarantees."),
  "event-loops-reactor-and-proactor": T("An event loop dispatches small units of work without letting one handler monopolize the loop. A reactor responds to readiness and initiates I/O; a proactor responds to completed operations. Both need state machines for partial messages, deadlines, cancellation, and backpressure. CPU-heavy work often belongs in a bounded worker pool.", "A readiness handler reads a request, parses only available bytes, and returns. A long image transformation is dispatched elsewhere so other connections remain responsive.", "Event|Socket ready|Reactor notified;Handler|Read and update parser|Short bounded work;Offload|Transform on worker|Loop continues serving events", "One event loop does not make shared state safe across multiple loop threads or worker callbacks.", "Why bound work per callback?", "A hot connection can otherwise starve timers and other sockets even though the system is technically nonblocking."),
  "buffering-and-backpressure": T("Buffers absorb temporary rate differences but cannot fix sustained arrival above service capacity. Backpressure slows or rejects producers before queues exhaust memory. Define queue limits in bytes as well as items, preserve cancellation, and propagate pressure across stages rather than moving the unbounded queue to another component.", "A producer sends 120 items/s to a consumer handling 100/s. An empty 200-item queue fills in 10 seconds under this constant-rate model.", "Start|Queue zero|Excess 20 per second;Five seconds|Queue 100|Half capacity;Ten seconds|Queue 200|Throttle or reject new work", "Increasing the queue delays overload and increases waiting time; it does not increase service capacity.", "How should a server handle a slow reader?", "Bound pending output, pause upstream production where possible, and apply a timeout or disconnect policy when limits are exceeded."),
  "event-driven-servers-in-production": T("Production event-driven servers combine nonblocking I/O with admission limits, timers, bounded callbacks, and graceful shutdown. They must budget per-connection state and queued output, handle half-closes, and avoid blocking libraries on the event thread. Observability should separate event-loop delay from downstream response time.", "One client sends a header one byte per second while another requests a large response but reads slowly. Apply independent header deadlines and output limits.", "Accept|Allocate bounded connection state|Admission check;Progress|Track header and output deadlines|No indefinite retention;Expire|Close stalled connection|Release buffers and descriptors", "Connection count alone misses memory consumption from large queued responses.", "What metric reveals a blocked event loop?", "Timer or heartbeat scheduling delay on the loop, correlated with callback duration and CPU profiles, distinguishes loop starvation from remote waiting."),
  "the-process-address-space": T("An address space contains mappings for code, static data, heap allocations, stacks, libraries, and mapped files. Each region has permissions and backing behavior. Virtual reservations need not consume physical pages immediately, and gaps can be unmapped. Layout varies with architecture, runtime, and address-space randomization.", "A program reserves a large region but touches only one page. Its virtual size can grow far more than its resident footprint.", "Reserve|Large virtual range|No full physical commitment;Touch|Write one page|Fault allocates backing;Inspect|Compare VSZ and RSS|Different memory quantities", "Heap growth and stack growth are conventions, not universal guarantees about all address layouts.", "Why does a bad pointer sometimes fault immediately?", "It may target an unmapped region or violate permissions; other invalid pointers can silently corrupt mapped memory."),
  "logical-and-physical-memory": T("A virtual or logical address belongs to a process's view; a physical address identifies memory accessible through hardware translation. Page tables map virtual pages to physical frames with permissions. Two processes can use the same virtual address for different data, or different virtual addresses for intentionally shared backing.", "Process A and B both access virtual address 0x1000. Their page tables map it to frames 8 and 12 respectively.", "A|Virtual page 1 maps frame 8|Private value;B|Virtual page 1 maps frame 12|Different value;MMU|Uses current translation context|Isolation preserved", "Virtual address equality across processes says nothing by itself about physical sharing.", "Can two virtual addresses refer to one frame?", "Yes. Aliasing and shared mappings are possible, subject to permissions and cache-coherence rules."),
  "contiguous-allocation-and-fragmentation": T("Contiguous allocation requires one sufficiently large adjacent range. External fragmentation leaves enough total free space split into unusable gaps; internal fragmentation wastes space inside allocated units. Compaction can consolidate movable allocations, while paging avoids requiring every process's physical memory to be contiguous. Allocator strategy influences fragmentation.", "Free holes of 4, 3, and 5 units total 12, but cannot satisfy a contiguous request for 6 without rearrangement or another allocation source.", "Free list|Holes 4 3 5|Total twelve;Request|Needs contiguous six|No single fit;Alternative|Compact or use pages|Trade movement for flexibility", "Total free memory alone does not prove a contiguous request can succeed.", "What does a larger allocation unit trade off?", "It can reduce metadata and mapping overhead while increasing internal waste for small requests."),
  "segmentation": T("Segmentation models memory as variable-sized logical regions with bases, bounds, and permissions. An address combines a segment selection with an offset, and the offset must fit the segment's limit. It matches logical structures but can suffer external fragmentation. Modern mainstream systems often rely primarily on paging rather than classic application-visible segmentation.", "A segment begins at physical 1000 with length 200. Offset 50 translates to 1050; offset 250 violates the bound.", "Select|Base 1000 limit 200|Segment metadata;Valid|Offset 50 becomes 1050|Within limit;Invalid|Offset 250 rejected|Protection fault", "Do not confuse a logical segmentation model with ELF loadable segments or every modern CPU's exact mode.", "How does paging differ?", "Paging uses fixed-sized units and page-table translation; segmentation uses variable-sized logical ranges with base-and-bound style reasoning."),
  "how-malloc-works": T("malloc manages user-space allocation metadata and obtains larger backing regions from the OS when needed. It can reuse free blocks, split regions, and maintain size classes or arenas. free returns ownership to the allocator but need not immediately return pages to the kernel. Fragmentation and allocator caching affect resident memory.", "Allocate 1,000 objects and free them. RSS may remain high because the allocator retains reusable pages or still-live objects pin partially occupied regions.", "Allocate|Reuse or request backing|Allocator owns region;Free|Object becomes reusable|Not necessarily unmapped;Reuse|New object uses old block|No new syscall required", "Using memory after free remains invalid even if the bytes appear unchanged.", "Why can freeing objects fail to reduce RSS?", "The allocator may cache pages, and fragmentation can prevent releasing a page that still contains live allocations."),
  "kernel-memory-allocation": T("Kernel allocators serve different needs: page-level allocation supplies physical pages, while slab-style caches efficiently reuse common object types. Allocation context matters because some callers may sleep or reclaim memory and others cannot. Physical contiguity, virtual contiguity, alignment, and DMA suitability are separate requirements.", "A filesystem repeatedly creates small inode-related objects. A reusable object cache avoids treating every request as a fresh general-purpose page allocation.", "Cache|Maintains reusable objects|Fixed object layout;Allocate|Take free object|Fast common path;Pressure|Refill or reclaim subject to context|Failure remains possible", "Kernel allocations can fail, especially under atomic-context or contiguity constraints.", "Why distinguish physical from virtual contiguity?", "A virtually contiguous range can map discontiguous frames, while some device operations require stricter physical or DMA mapping guarantees."),
  "memory-metrics": T("VSZ describes virtual mappings, RSS counts resident pages attributed to a process, and PSS proportionally divides shared resident pages among sharers. These measure different things and cannot be added or compared blindly. Private dirty memory, swap, and kernel accounting provide additional evidence for actual pressure and leak diagnosis.", "Two processes each have 20 MiB private and share 10 MiB resident code. Each RSS is about 30 MiB, while each PSS is about 25 MiB in this simplified model.", "Private|20 MiB per process|Forty total;Shared|10 MiB counted in both RSS|RSS sum sixty;PSS|Split shared pages equally|PSS sum fifty", "Summing RSS double-counts shared pages and is not a precise system-wide physical usage total.", "Which metric helps attribute shared memory fairly?", "PSS apportions shared pages, but interpret it alongside private memory and kernel or container accounting."),
  "memory-pressure-and-the-oom-killer": T("Under pressure the kernel can reclaim caches, write back dirty pages, and swap eligible anonymous memory when configured. If required allocations still cannot succeed, an out-of-memory mechanism may terminate a task. Container limits can trigger local failures even when the host has free memory. Reclaim stalls can hurt latency before any kill occurs.", "A service stays below host RAM capacity but exceeds its cgroup memory limit during a burst. Inspect local memory events rather than assuming a machine-wide shortage.", "Grow|Working set increases|Local limit approaches;Reclaim|Try freeing eligible pages|Latency may rise;OOM|Limit cannot be satisfied|Victim or allocation failure", "A high cache value is not automatically a leak; reclaimable and pinned memory behave differently.", "What should an OOM investigation collect?", "Limit settings, memory events, private and shared growth, workload changes, and timestamps tying the kill to request or batch activity."),
  "why-virtual-memory-exists": T("Virtual memory gives each process a controlled address-space abstraction independent of physical placement. It supports isolation, sparse mappings, shared pages, relocation, and demand population. It is useful even without swap. The abstraction requires translation metadata and faults, so it introduces costs as well as flexibility.", "A database maps a large file but reads a small region. Only accessed pages need immediate residency, subject to caching and readahead behavior.", "Map|Large virtual region|Backing file identified;Access|Touch selected page|Populate if absent;Share|Other mapping may reuse page|Permissions still enforced", "Virtual memory is not simply extra RAM obtained from disk; swapping is only one related mechanism.", "Why can a 64-bit process reserve far more than RAM?", "Address reservation and physical residency are different commitments; actual access can still fail or trigger reclaim under pressure."),
  "the-mmu-and-address-translation": T("The MMU translates virtual addresses through cached translations or page-table walks and enforces access permissions. Split an address into virtual page number and page offset; translation replaces the page number with a frame number while retaining the offset. Invalid or prohibited mappings trigger an exception for the OS to handle.", "With 4 KiB pages, virtual address 0x1234 is page 1 plus offset 0x234. If page 1 maps to frame 9, the physical address is 0x9234.", "Split|VPN 1 and offset 0x234|Page size 4096;Translate|VPN 1 maps frame 9|Permissions checked;Combine|Frame base 0x9000 plus offset|Physical 0x9234", "A page-table entry contains more than an address; present, writable, executable, and other attributes matter.", "Does translation change the offset?", "For ordinary fixed-size paging, the within-page offset is preserved; the page-to-frame mapping changes the base."),
  "paging": T("Paging divides virtual memory into fixed-sized pages and physical memory into corresponding frames. Page tables permit noncontiguous physical placement while preserving contiguous virtual ranges. The final partially used page causes internal fragmentation; smaller pages reduce that waste but require more translation entries for a given mapped size.", "A 10 KiB region uses three 4 KiB pages in a simple allocation model, leaving 2 KiB unused in the last page.", "Size|Request 10 KiB|Page size 4 KiB;Allocate|Three frames required|12 KiB capacity;Waste|Last page uses 2 KiB|2 KiB internal fragmentation", "Page size is platform/configuration dependent, and huge-page mappings change the arithmetic.", "Why avoid one flat page table for a huge sparse space?", "It would reserve metadata for vast unmapped regions; hierarchical tables allocate lower levels only where mappings are needed."),
  "multilevel-page-tables": T("Hierarchical page tables split a virtual page number into indices for successive levels. Missing branches represent unmapped large regions without allocating every leaf entry. This saves metadata for sparse address spaces but can require multiple memory accesses on a translation-cache miss. Large-page entries can terminate a walk earlier.", "In a toy two-level system, virtual page bits select directory entry 3 and leaf entry 5. The leaf provides a frame and permissions.", "Root|Index 3 selects leaf table|Branch must exist;Leaf|Index 5 selects mapping|Frame and flags;Access|Combine frame with offset|Or fault if absent", "Do not use toy index widths as universal architecture constants.", "Why is a TLB valuable with multiple levels?", "A cached translation avoids repeating the page-table walk for each access to the same mapped page."),
  "the-tlb": T("A translation lookaside buffer caches recent address translations and permissions. A TLB miss usually triggers a page-table walk, not necessarily a page fault. When mappings or permissions change, stale translations must be invalidated appropriately, including on other CPUs that could use them. Address-space identifiers can reduce unnecessary invalidation.", "A page is changed from writable to read-only while another core has cached the old permissions. Coordinate invalidation before relying on the new protection.", "Cached|Core B remembers writable mapping|Old permission;Update|Kernel changes page table|Cache may remain stale;Invalidate|Relevant TLB entries removed|New access rechecks mapping", "A TLB miss and a missing resident page are different events with different costs.", "What is a TLB shootdown?", "It is coordination to invalidate relevant translations on other CPUs, ensuring they stop using mappings that have changed."),
  "page-faults": T("A page fault occurs when translation or permission checks cannot complete the access normally. The kernel distinguishes valid lazy population, copy-on-write, and other recoverable cases from illegal access. A fault may require no disk I/O or may wait on storage. The outcome depends on mapping metadata, not merely the fact that a fault occurred.", "First writing a valid anonymous page may allocate a zero-filled frame; writing an unmapped address instead produces an invalid-access outcome.", "Access|Translation cannot complete|Fault raised;Classify|Check mapping and permissions|Valid demand or illegal access;Resolve|Populate and retry or signal|Different outcomes", "Major and minor fault counters need platform-specific interpretation; not every fault reads disk.", "Can a read-only mapping fault on a write?", "Yes. It may be a legitimate copy-on-write case or a forbidden write, depending on the mapping's intended semantics."),
  "demand-paging": T("Demand paging delays obtaining a page's backing until it is accessed. This reduces startup work and memory use for untouched regions, but moves latency to the first access. Readahead and prefaulting trade extra work and residency for fewer later stalls. Access patterns determine whether that trade is worthwhile.", "A service maps a 1 GiB dataset but queries only its first 4 KiB page. Demand loading avoids reading the entire dataset just to answer that query.", "Map|Reserve dataset mapping|Pages not all resident;First query|Touch first page|Fault may load data;Repeat|Page remains resident|Access can avoid storage", "A fast map call is not proof that later random accesses will be fast.", "When might prefaulting help?", "For a known hot working set with strict startup-to-serving boundaries, provided memory capacity and startup cost are acceptable."),
  "page-replacement-algorithms": T("Replacement chooses which resident page to evict when capacity is limited. FIFO uses arrival order, LRU approximates recent use, Clock uses reference information, and optimal replacement is a theoretical future-aware baseline. Dirty victims can require write-back. Real kernels use richer policies than a textbook exact-LRU list.", "With two frames and accesses A,B,A,C, exact LRU evicts B when C arrives because A was used more recently.", "A then B|Frames A B|Both fault;Access A|A becomes most recent|Hit;Access C|Evict B|Frames A C", "FIFO can exhibit Belady's anomaly; adding frames is not guaranteed to reduce faults under every policy.", "Why is exact LRU expensive?", "Tracking every access precisely adds metadata and synchronization work; hardware reference bits support cheaper approximations."),
  "working-sets-and-thrashing": T("A working set is the actively reused collection of pages over a chosen interval. When competing working sets exceed available memory, the system can repeatedly evict pages that will soon be needed again. This thrashing spends time faulting and reclaiming rather than doing useful work; reducing concurrency can improve throughput.", "Three workers each repeatedly touch 100 MiB on a machine with only 200 MiB available for their data. Running fewer workers may keep active sets resident.", "Demand|300 MiB active data|Capacity 200 MiB;Evict|Needed pages replaced|Repeated faults;Limit|Run fewer workers|More useful work per second", "Low CPU utilization during thrashing is not evidence that more workers are needed.", "How do you distinguish a leak from a large working set?", "Track retained private memory and reuse over time; a stable but oversized active set differs from unbounded growth."),
  "copy-on-write": T("Copy-on-write lets mappings initially share physical pages while delaying duplication until a write requires private state. The OS marks relevant mappings nonwritable, handles the write fault, and creates or reuses a private writable page as appropriate. It saves memory for unchanged data but can make write-heavy fork patterns expensive.", "Parent and child share page P after fork. The child writes one byte, causing a page-granularity private copy rather than a one-byte allocation.", "Fork|Both map frame P|Read-only COW state;Child write|Fault requests private version|Copy page if shared;Resume|Child maps frame Q|Parent still sees P", "Copy-on-write is not free: page copies, faults, and page-table work can create latency spikes.", "Why can a tiny write increase memory by a page?", "Protection and sharing are managed at page granularity, so the private backing unit is larger than the modified byte."),
  "mmap-and-memory-mapped-files": T("mmap associates a virtual range with a file or anonymous backing. File accesses then use ordinary loads and stores, with faults populating pages as needed. Shared and private mappings have different write visibility; persistence and synchronization require explicit attention. File truncation and mapping lifetime introduce hazards absent from a simple copied buffer.", "Map a file privately and change a byte. The process sees its private modification, while the underlying file is not updated by that private store.", "Map|MAP_PRIVATE file region|File-backed initial data;Write|Create private modified page|COW semantics;Observe|File unchanged by store|Process view differs", "Memory mapping does not eliminate I/O errors or guarantee durable writes from shared mappings.", "When might read be simpler?", "For bounded sequential processing, explicit buffers can make error handling and memory ownership clearer than fault-driven access."),
  "shared-libraries-and-shared-pages": T("Processes can share immutable executable and library pages while keeping writable state private. Dynamic linking resolves symbol references and may perform relocations. Position-independent code helps sharing by reducing modifications to code pages. Physical sharing depends on backing objects and mappings, not merely equal byte contents.", "Ten processes use one library's read-only code. Those pages may be resident once, while each process still needs its own mutable globals and stack.", "Load|Map common library object|Shared backing;Execute|Read immutable instructions|Physical pages reusable;Mutate|Update process-private global|Separate writable state", "A library loaded into many processes does not mean all of its memory is shared.", "Why does PSS help here?", "It apportions shared resident pages rather than counting the entire shared library footprint in every process's total."),
  "huge-pages-numa-and-aslr": T("Huge pages cover more memory per translation and can reduce TLB pressure, but increase allocation granularity and complicate reclaim. NUMA makes access cost depend on the memory's node relative to the CPU. ASLR randomizes address layout to make exploitation harder. These solve different performance and protection problems.", "A database thread moves to another NUMA node while its large buffer remains on the original node. Translation efficiency can improve with huge pages even as remote-memory latency worsens.", "Place|Buffer allocated on node 0|Local initial access;Migrate|Thread runs on node 1|Remote memory traffic;Evaluate|Measure TLB and NUMA effects|Separate causes", "Huge pages do not fix poor NUMA placement, and ASLR does not prevent memory corruption itself.", "What would you measure before enabling huge pages?", "Translation misses, allocation success, fragmentation, memory footprint, and latency under the actual workload."),
  "the-file-abstraction": T("A regular file provides a named persistent byte sequence rather than exposing raw device sectors. The OS associates metadata, permissions, size, and allocation with it. Not every file-like descriptor is seekable: sockets, pipes, and devices can share read/write interfaces while obeying different semantics.", "A parser reads from either a regular file or a pipe. It must not assume seek is available just because both inputs use a descriptor.", "Open|Obtain file-like descriptor|Object type matters;Read|Receive bytes|Partial reads possible;Seek|Works for regular file|Pipe may reject operation", "Uniform APIs do not imply identical capabilities or durability guarantees.", "Why design a streaming parser?", "It handles partial input and nonseekable sources without requiring the entire file or message in memory."),
  "file-descriptors": T("A Unix file descriptor is a small integer indexing a process's descriptor table. Entries refer to open file descriptions or other kernel objects. dup and fork can create references sharing an offset; separate opens usually create independent open descriptions. Closing one descriptor does not necessarily destroy the underlying object while other references remain.", "Open a file, duplicate its descriptor, then read through each. Both descriptors share the same open-description offset in this example.", "open|Descriptor 3 offset zero|New open description;dup|Descriptor 4 references same object|Shared offset;read|Read via 3 advances offset|4 observes advanced position", "The integer 3 in another process need not name the same object.", "Why use close-on-exec?", "It prevents unintended descriptor inheritance into a replacement program, reducing leaks and accidental resource retention."),
  "file-operations": T("open establishes access, read and write transfer bytes, seek changes a seekable object's offset, and close releases a descriptor reference. pread and pwrite specify an offset without modifying the shared file position. Every operation needs result checking; partial transfers and delayed write errors are part of normal robust design.", "Two threads read independent records from one file. pread at explicit offsets avoids racing through a shared seek-plus-read sequence.", "Thread A|pread offset 0|Independent position;Thread B|pread offset 4096|No shared seek update;Results|Check actual byte counts|Handle short record or error", "seek followed by read is not an atomic positional read when another thread shares the offset.", "Why might read return fewer bytes than requested?", "EOF, available stream data, interruption, and object-specific behavior can limit progress; callers must follow the API's return contract."),
  "directories-and-path-resolution": T("A directory maps names to filesystem objects. Resolving a path walks components from a root or working directory, checking traversal permissions and handling links or mount points. Relative paths depend on context. Directory-descriptor-based APIs can anchor lookup and reduce races from changing working directories or path components.", "A service checks a path and later opens it; another actor replaces a component between those operations. Prefer a securely constrained open rather than trusting the earlier check.", "Check|Path points to expected object|Momentary observation;Change|Component replaced|Check becomes stale;Open|Resolve again under policy|Must enforce constraints now", "Existence checks followed by open can create time-of-check/time-of-use races.", "Why is directory execute permission important?", "On Unix it controls search/traversal through the directory, distinct from permission to list its names."),
  "inodes": T("An inode represents filesystem object metadata and references to its stored content; directory entries provide names that refer to it. Multiple hard links can name the same inode. Open references can keep an unlinked file alive. Inode numbers are meaningful within a filesystem and can be reused after deletion.", "A log file is unlinked while a process keeps it open. Its name disappears, but disk blocks may remain allocated until the final reference closes.", "Open|Descriptor references inode|Name also exists;Unlink|Remove directory entry|Open reference remains;Close|Last reference released|Storage may be reclaimed", "Deleting a filename does not always immediately release its storage.", "Why can df and directory totals disagree?", "Open unlinked files, metadata, reserved space, and other filesystem accounting differences can consume blocks not visible in a simple name walk."),
  "hard-and-symbolic-links": T("A hard link is another directory entry for the same filesystem object, while a symbolic link stores a path to resolve later. Hard links generally cannot cross filesystem boundaries; symlinks can point across them and can dangle. Relative symlink targets are interpreted relative to the link's containing directory.", "Create names A and B for one inode, plus symlink C pointing to A. Removing A leaves B usable but can leave C dangling.", "Before|A and B share inode|C points to path A;Unlink A|B still references object|Object survives;Resolve C|Target A absent|Dangling symlink", "A symlink does not increase the target inode's hard-link count.", "Why do hard links survive renaming another name?", "They reference the underlying object independently; they are not path redirects through the other name."),
  "file-permissions": T("Unix mode bits specify owner, group, and other read/write/execute permissions, with different meanings for files and directories. ACLs can add rules, and umask influences creation defaults. Deleting a name generally depends on its parent directory's permissions and special rules such as the sticky bit, not merely the file's writable bit.", "A read-only file can still be removed by a user allowed to modify its parent directory, subject to ownership and sticky-directory constraints.", "File|Mode denies content writes|Read-only data;Directory|Allows name modification|Deletion permission path;Policy|Check sticky bit and identity|Final authorization", "chmod alone is not the entire access policy; ACLs, capabilities, mount settings, and security modules may also matter.", "Why separate directory write and search rights?", "Changing names and traversing to objects are different operations, and both may be needed for a requested action."),
  "the-vfs-layer": T("The virtual filesystem layer provides common interfaces over different filesystem implementations. It dispatches operations through generic objects such as files, inodes, dentries, and mounts to the appropriate backend. This allows similar APIs for local, network, and pseudo filesystems without promising identical latency, persistence, or failure behavior.", "The same read API accesses a local file and a network-mounted file. The latter may wait on a remote server even though application syntax is unchanged.", "Application|Calls read|Common descriptor API;VFS|Dispatches filesystem operation|Backend-specific method;Backend|Local device or remote server|Different latency and failures", "An identical interface does not erase consistency or availability differences between filesystems.", "Why keep a generic filesystem layer?", "It centralizes namespace and object integration while letting implementations provide their own storage and operation logic."),
  "file-allocation-and-extents": T("Filesystems map logical file ranges to storage blocks. An extent represents a contiguous run, reducing metadata for large sequential allocations. Fragmentation increases the number of mappings and can hurt locality, especially on rotating storage. Sparse files represent holes without allocating every logical byte, so apparent size can exceed allocated space.", "A 1 GiB sparse file contains only one written 4 KiB region. Its logical length is large while allocated blocks can remain much smaller.", "Create|Logical length 1 GiB|Holes need no full allocation;Write|Populate one region|Allocate backing blocks;Inspect|Compare size and allocated space|Different measures", "Sparse allocation is not guaranteed to remain sparse after every copy or transformation.", "Why are extents efficient?", "One mapping describes many adjacent blocks, reducing metadata traversal and supporting larger sequential I/O."),
  "dentry-and-inode-caches": T("Directory-entry caches accelerate name lookup, while inode caches retain object metadata. Cached negative lookups can record that a name was absent. Cache hits avoid repeated filesystem work, but memory pressure and remote coherency rules affect retention and validity. Cached metadata is distinct from cached file contents.", "Repeatedly open the same deep path. Warm name and inode caches can make later opens faster even when no file data has been read.", "Cold lookup|Walk directory components|Metadata work;Cache|Retain names and inodes|Lookup state warm;Repeat|Resolve with cached entries|Less backend work", "A warm page cache and a warm path lookup cache are different performance conditions.", "Why benchmark cold and warm cases separately?", "They exercise different storage and metadata paths; mixing them can hide startup cost or overstate steady-state latency."),
  "crash-consistency": T("Crash consistency asks which filesystem states are recoverable after interruption between writes. Multi-block updates can leave metadata and data disagreeing unless ordering, logging, or copy-on-write protocols preserve invariants. Atomic visibility of a rename is distinct from durability after power loss. Applications must define their acceptable recovery state.", "Replace a configuration by writing a temporary file, syncing it, renaming it, and syncing the containing directory as required by the filesystem and platform.", "Prepare|Write complete temporary content|Old name remains valid;Persist|Sync file then rename|New name becomes visible;Commit|Sync directory when required|Name change recovery boundary", "A journaled filesystem does not automatically make every multi-file application update transactional.", "What invariant should recovery preserve?", "Readers should find a complete old or new version according to the protocol, rather than a partially written configuration."),
  "journaling": T("Journaling records intended filesystem changes in a recoverable log before applying or completing their home-location updates. Recovery can replay committed records and ignore incomplete transactions according to the design. Metadata-only and data-journaling modes protect different content. Logging adds writes but simplifies bounded recovery.", "A metadata update is logged and committed before a crash interrupts copying it to its final location. Recovery can replay the committed transaction.", "Log|Write transaction description|Not committed yet;Commit|Persist commit marker|Recovery may replay;Recover|Apply committed metadata|Ignore incomplete transaction", "Journal presence does not imply that recently written user data was included or persisted.", "Why distinguish commit from checkpoint?", "Commit establishes recoverability through the log; checkpointing places changes in their home locations and can reclaim log space."),
  "copy-on-write-file-systems": T("A copy-on-write filesystem writes modified blocks to new locations and then publishes updated references. Old versions can remain reachable for snapshots. This avoids overwriting the only valid version during an update, but introduces allocation, fragmentation, and reference-management costs. Checksums and redundancy are separate design choices, not automatic consequences of COW.", "Modify one block in a snapshotted file. The new tree references a new block while the snapshot retains the old block.", "Snapshot|Old root references block A|Stable historical view;Modify|Write new block B|A remains intact;Publish|New root references B|Snapshot still references A", "Snapshots retain referenced storage and are not independent off-machine backups.", "Why can deleting a large file free little space?", "Snapshots or clones may still reference its blocks, so physical reclamation waits until those references disappear."),
  "storage-hardware": T("HDDs pay mechanical seek and rotational costs; SSDs use flash with erase-before-rewrite constraints and internal translation; NVMe is an interface supporting efficient queues, not a distinct persistence guarantee. Controllers, caches, firmware, and power-loss protection affect observed behavior. Sequential and random workloads stress devices differently.", "Compare many 4 KiB random reads with one large sequential read. A rotating disk must reposition repeatedly, while flash avoids mechanical seeks but still has internal limits.", "Request|Random block sequence|Poor spatial locality;Device|Seek or flash translation|Hardware-dependent cost;Measure|IOPS and tail latency|Separate workload shapes", "An SSD does not make every write equally fast or guarantee survival of unflushed volatile cache data.", "Why can SSD latency spike?", "Garbage collection, queueing, write amplification, thermal limits, and firmware work can delay individual operations."),
  "storage-performance": T("Storage performance combines IOPS, transfer throughput, latency distribution, and queue depth. For fixed-size operations, throughput approximately equals IOPS times bytes per operation. More outstanding requests can increase utilization until saturation, after which queueing mainly increases latency. Reads, writes, access patterns, and durability settings must be reported with results.", "10,000 operations/s at 4 KiB correspond to about 39.1 MiB/s, not 10,000 MiB/s. Compare this with a large-block sequential workload.", "Rate|10000 operations each second|4096 bytes each;Multiply|40960000 bytes per second|About 39.1 MiB/s;Queue|Raise depth cautiously|Watch p99 latency", "Average latency hides long outliers, and cached reads may not measure the device at all.", "How does Little's law help?", "For a stable system, average outstanding work equals completion rate times average latency; use consistent units and boundaries."),
  "disk-scheduling-algorithms": T("FCFS serves requests in arrival order; shortest-seek-first favors nearby tracks; SCAN sweeps in a direction; C-SCAN returns to one end for a more uniform directional service pattern. These illustrate mechanical positioning trade-offs. Modern block schedulers also consider fairness and deadlines, and SSDs have different seek economics.", "A disk head at track 50 receives requests for 10, 55, and 90. Shortest-seek-first initially chooses 55, while a sweep policy depends on its current direction.", "Queue|Tracks 10 55 90|Head at fifty;SSTF|Choose 55|Distance five;Fairness|Distant request may wait|Need starvation consideration", "Shortest seek can starve distant requests under a sustained nearby stream.", "Why does a scheduling answer need direction?", "SCAN-style algorithms use the current sweep direction; without it, the next request order is underspecified."),
  "the-block-layer": T("The block layer mediates filesystem requests and storage drivers, representing operations against block-addressed devices. It may merge adjacent requests, queue work, and dispatch through hardware queues. Ordering and flush requirements constrain optimization. Device-mapper layers can add logical volumes or other transformations before requests reach physical hardware.", "Two adjacent writes may be merged into a larger request, but a durability barrier can prevent reordering that would violate the caller's persistence protocol.", "Filesystem|Submits block requests|Logical ranges;Block layer|Queues and possibly merges|Respect ordering flags;Driver|Dispatches device work|Reports completion", "A device completion timestamp alone does not explain time already spent waiting above the device.", "Why trace multiple layers?", "Application waiting, filesystem work, block queues, and device service contribute different portions of end-to-end I/O latency."),
  "the-page-cache": T("The page cache holds file-backed data in memory so repeated reads can avoid device access. Buffered writes dirty cached pages; write-back later sends them to storage. Dirty-page limits can throttle writers, and reclaim must account for whether pages can be discarded or need persistence first. Cached data remains subject to memory pressure.", "A file is read twice. The second read can hit memory; a later write updates cached data before the background flush completes.", "Cold read|Fetch file page|Populate cache;Warm read|Reuse cached page|Avoid device read;Write|Mark page dirty|Write-back still pending", "Dropping caches changes the workload and should not be a routine production tuning shortcut.", "Why are dirty pages harder to reclaim?", "Their latest contents cannot simply be discarded; the system generally needs to write them back or preserve them through another valid mechanism."),
  "buffered-and-direct-io": T("Buffered I/O uses the page cache, benefiting repeated access and write coalescing. Direct I/O attempts to bypass selected caching paths and often imposes alignment constraints. It can help applications that manage their own caches, but does not automatically provide durability or universally better throughput. Exact behavior depends on filesystem and platform.", "A database already maintains a large buffer pool. Compare double caching through buffered I/O with aligned direct requests under the same workload.", "Buffered|Application plus page cache|Potential duplicate caching;Direct|Aligned device-facing request|Application manages reuse;Evaluate|Measure memory and latency|Durability still explicit", "O_DIRECT and fsync solve different problems; bypassing cache is not the same as committing data to stable media.", "When might buffered I/O win?", "For small repeated reads or workloads benefiting from kernel readahead and coalescing without application-managed caching."),
  "durability-and-fsync": T("Durability requires defining which data must survive which failures. fsync requests persistence of file data and required metadata according to the platform contract; directory updates can require their own sync. fdatasync can avoid some unrelated metadata work. Group commit amortizes flush cost while expanding the batch's waiting window.", "A service acknowledges an order only after its log record and required durability operation succeed. A crash before that point must not be treated as a confirmed commit.", "Append|Record copied to buffer|Not yet durable;Sync|Wait for required persistence|Check returned error;Acknowledge|Report committed operation|Recovery can find record", "Ignoring sync errors can turn an apparently successful transaction into silent data loss.", "Why batch fsync calls?", "One durable flush can cover several records, improving throughput, but acknowledgement latency and the uncommitted window must be explicit."),
  "raid": T("RAID combines devices for performance, availability, or both. RAID 0 stripes without redundancy; RAID 1 mirrors; RAID 5 uses single parity; RAID 6 uses double parity; RAID 10 stripes mirrored pairs. Usable capacity, write overhead, rebuild exposure, and failure tolerance differ. Redundancy does not protect against deletion or every correlated failure.", "Four equal 1 TB disks in RAID 10 provide roughly 2 TB usable before formatting. Losing one disk is tolerated; losing both members of one mirror is not.", "Layout|Two mirrored pairs|Striped across pairs;Failure|One member lost|Mirror supplies data;Second failure|Same pair loses other member|Data unavailable", "RAID is not a backup and does not guarantee integrity without appropriate checks and recovery procedures.", "Why can rebuilds be risky?", "They stress remaining devices while redundancy is reduced, increasing exposure to another failure or unreadable blocks."),
  "diagnosing-io-problems": T("Diagnose I/O by separating application demand, filesystem/cache behavior, queueing, and device service. Correlate syscall duration with iostat-style latency and queue metrics, dirty-page activity, and workload changes. High device utilization has different meaning on single-queue disks and parallel storage. Compare read and write paths independently.", "Latency rises during a write burst. Check whether writers are throttled on dirty pages or whether requests are waiting in an overloaded device queue.", "Observe|Request latency rises|Timestamped workload;Correlate|Dirty pages and device queues|Locate waiting;Experiment|Reduce write concurrency|Test bottleneck hypothesis", "One utilization percentage cannot prove that every available device execution resource is saturated.", "What evidence would justify faster storage?", "Repeated workload-correlated device service or queueing limits after eliminating application locking, cache effects, and unnecessary I/O."),
  "protection-vs-security": T("Protection mechanisms control access among subjects and objects: address boundaries, permissions, and capabilities are examples. Security includes broader goals such as resisting malicious behavior, authenticating identities, reducing attack surface, and maintaining integrity. A correct permission check can still be undermined by a privileged application's logic flaw.", "A service runs with permission to read every file but only needs one directory. Reduce its authority so a parser bug has a smaller reachable damage scope.", "Identify|Service needs one directory|Required authority;Constrain|Grant narrow access|Protection boundary;Review|Test hostile inputs and dependencies|Broader security work", "Running as a non-root user is useful but not a complete security design.", "What is least privilege?", "Grant only the operations and resources needed for the current task, and avoid retaining unnecessary authority after setup."),
  "users-groups-and-permissions": T("User and group identities participate in discretionary access checks. Effective credentials can differ from the identity that originally launched a process, and supplementary groups add access paths. Service accounts separate application ownership from interactive users. Permission reasoning must include parent directories, ACLs, and the credentials actually used at the check.", "A worker belongs to a group allowed to read reports but not modify them. Verify effective groups and directory traversal rights before changing file modes.", "Identity|Inspect effective user and groups|Actual credentials;Path|Check directory traversal|Reach target;Object|Apply read authorization|Grant or deny", "Adding broad world-writable permissions hides the underlying ownership mistake and increases exposure.", "Why use separate service accounts?", "They allow narrower resource ownership and make one compromised service less able to modify another service's files."),
  "access-control-and-capabilities": T("Discretionary access control follows owner-managed permissions; mandatory controls enforce system policy; role-based control grants through roles; attribute-based control evaluates contextual properties. Capabilities represent specific authority rather than unrestricted privilege. Linux capabilities split some root powers, while object-capability designs use references as authority; these are related but distinct concepts.", "A server needs to bind a restricted port but should not retain broad administrative powers. Grant only the relevant capability or delegate socket creation.", "Need|Bind listening port|Specific privileged action;Grant|Narrow capability or passed socket|Reduced authority;Drop|Remove setup privilege afterward|Smaller runtime exposure", "A capability name alone does not prove safe confinement; inherited privileges and accessible objects also matter.", "Why prefer an opened descriptor to broad path access?", "It can grant authority over one already selected object without allowing arbitrary namespace traversal."),
  "memory-protection": T("Memory protection enforces readable, writable, executable, and privilege-level constraints on mappings. Guard pages detect some overflows; nonexecutable data pages hinder injected-code execution; address isolation limits cross-process access. These reduce consequences or detect violations but do not replace bounds checks and safe ownership in the application.", "A stack grows into a guard page. The access faults instead of silently overwriting an adjacent mapped region in this protected layout.", "Mapping|Guard page has no access|Boundary established;Overflow|Program touches guard|Permission violation;Fault|Kernel reports invalid access|Failure becomes observable", "Memory protection cannot detect every out-of-bounds access that stays within a valid writable page.", "Why use write-xor-execute policies?", "They reduce mappings that are simultaneously writable and executable, making some code-injection paths harder while requiring care for JIT runtimes."),
  "seccomp-and-syscall-filtering": T("Linux seccomp filtering restricts the system calls a process may invoke, often with checks on numeric arguments. It narrows the kernel interface reachable after compromise but is not a complete path-based authorization system. Filters must match architecture and runtime behavior, and deployment should test startup, steady state, and error paths.", "A worker that only processes an already opened input should not need to create arbitrary network connections. Apply a tested syscall policy after required setup.", "Setup|Open required resources|Broader initialization needs;Filter|Restrict allowed syscalls|Reduced kernel surface;Violation|Forbidden call is handled by policy|Fail visibly", "Seccomp cannot safely inspect arbitrary pointed-to strings as though it were a pathname policy engine.", "Why trace error paths before enforcing a filter?", "Libraries may use additional syscalls during DNS failures, logging, cleanup, or allocation, so happy-path tracing alone misses required behavior."),
  "resource-limits": T("Resource limits bound consumption such as open descriptors, address space, or CPU time, while cgroups control aggregate resource use across task groups. Soft and hard limits have different adjustment rules. Limits protect capacity but can turn growth into explicit errors; applications need graceful handling instead of assuming every allocation or open succeeds.", "A worker reaches its descriptor limit and accept fails. Stop admission, release leaked resources, and preserve capacity for diagnostics.", "Grow|Descriptors approach soft limit|Headroom shrinking;Open|Limit reached|EMFILE possible;Recover|Close leak and bound admission|Capacity restored", "Raising a limit can mask a leak and shift failure to system-wide exhaustion.", "Why reserve diagnostic capacity?", "During exhaustion even logging or opening a status file may fail; bounded resource use and prearranged observability keep failures diagnosable."),
  "common-os-vulnerabilities": T("Common failure classes include memory corruption, use-after-free, privilege mistakes, unsafe temporary files, and time-of-check/time-of-use races. Attackers exploit a mismatch between assumed and actual ownership or validation boundaries. Defensive design combines memory-safe interfaces, narrow privileges, atomic operations, and keeping platform components maintained.", "A service validates a path and then reopens it after an attacker replaces a symlink. The second lookup may reach a different object.", "Validate|Path appears allowed|Temporary fact;Race|Namespace changes|Assumption invalid;Operate|Use constrained atomic open|Enforce policy at use", "A prior string check does not prove the object ultimately opened is safe.", "How do you prevent a TOCTOU path race?", "Use suitable descriptor-relative and resolution-constraining APIs, validate the opened object, and avoid separating authorization from the actual operation."),
  "side-channels": T("Side channels reveal information through observable effects such as timing, cache activity, or resource contention rather than direct access to protected data. A secret-dependent branch or memory access can change those effects. Constant-time algorithms, isolation, and platform mitigations reduce specific channels, but threat assumptions must be explicit.", "A naive secret comparison stops at the first mismatch. Repeated timing observations can reveal prefix-dependent behavior even though no secret bytes are returned.", "Compare|Stop at first mismatch|Work depends on prefix;Observe|Measure many response times|Noise can be averaged;Mitigate|Use vetted constant-time primitive|Avoid secret-dependent path", "Adding random delay is not a reliable substitute for removing the underlying secret-dependent behavior.", "Why are microbenchmarks insufficient proof?", "Compilers, CPUs, caches, and deployment conditions can introduce leakage; use reviewed primitives and a defined threat model."),
  "what-virtualization-is": T("Virtualization presents an abstract machine or resource interface independent of a particular physical allocation. Hardware virtualization lets guests run their own operating systems, while process virtual memory and runtime virtual machines operate at other boundaries. The abstraction enables consolidation and migration but adds scheduling, translation, and device mediation layers.", "Two guest systems share one physical host. Each sees virtual CPUs, but four virtual CPUs per guest do not imply eight dedicated physical cores.", "Guest|Sees virtual CPU allocation|Abstract machine;Host|Schedules vCPU work|Shared physical capacity;Load|Guests compete|Performance isolation matters", "Configured virtual capacity is not always reserved physical capacity.", "Why can a guest show unexplained waiting?", "Its vCPU may be runnable inside the guest while the host is not scheduling it, adding an outer scheduling delay."),
  "virtual-machines-and-hypervisors": T("A hypervisor manages guest execution and virtual hardware boundaries. Type-1 and type-2 labels describe broad deployment styles, but modern implementations can blur them. Hardware assistance handles many privileged transitions, while paravirtual devices reduce emulation overhead. Guest isolation still depends on the hypervisor, device stack, and configuration.", "A guest submits disk I/O through a virtual device. Trace the guest driver, host backend, and physical device rather than attributing all latency to the guest filesystem.", "Guest|Issues virtual disk request|Guest driver;Hypervisor|Routes through backend|Host resource scheduling;Device|Completes physical work|Result returned to guest", "A VM boundary is stronger than shared process memory but is not an absolute guarantee against all host or device flaws.", "Why use paravirtual drivers?", "They provide an interface designed for virtualization and can avoid expensive imitation of legacy hardware behavior."),
  "virtualized-memory": T("Guest virtual addresses translate to guest physical addresses, which are then mapped to host physical memory. Nested page-table hardware can accelerate this two-stage model, but translation and host reclaim still affect performance. Ballooning and host swapping change available backing; guest memory metrics do not reveal every host-level pressure source.", "A guest has free pages according to its own view while the host is overcommitted. Host-side paging can still increase guest access latency.", "Guest VA|Translate through guest tables|Guest physical page;Nested map|Resolve host frame|Second translation layer;Host pressure|Backing may be delayed|Guest latency increases", "Guest physical memory is not necessarily a fixed dedicated host frame for the VM's entire lifetime.", "What should you correlate during VM memory stalls?", "Guest faults and reclaim with host pressure, ballooning, swapping, and placement metrics."),
  "containers-vs-virtual-machines": T("Containers isolate process views and resource groups while sharing the host kernel. Virtual machines run separate guest kernels behind virtual hardware. Containers can start quickly and share image layers, while VMs provide a different kernel isolation boundary. Neither model automatically guarantees fair capacity or safe configuration.", "A Linux container reports the host kernel family despite carrying its own user-space libraries. A VM can boot a different guest kernel.", "Container|Own user-space filesystem|Shared host kernel;VM|Own guest kernel|Virtual hardware boundary;Choose|Compare isolation and density|Workload requirements decide", "A container image is not a complete independent kernel environment.", "Why can a kernel feature be missing inside a container?", "The host kernel and runtime policy determine availability; packaging a newer user-space library cannot create absent kernel support."),
  "namespaces": T("Linux namespaces give tasks different views of resources such as process IDs, mounts, networks, hostnames, IPC, users, and cgroups. They partition visibility or identity, while separate controls govern consumption and permissions. A task can have different identifiers as observed from different PID namespaces.", "A process appears as PID 1 inside a container and as a different PID on the host. Both observations refer to the same underlying task.", "Host|Task has host PID|Outer view;Namespace|Task appears as PID 1|Inner view;Inspect|Map views intentionally|Avoid mistaken identity", "Namespaces alone do not impose CPU or memory limits.", "What complements namespaces for resource control?", "Cgroups account for and constrain groups of tasks; capability and security policies further restrict authority."),
  "cgroups": T("Control groups organize tasks for resource accounting and controls such as CPU bandwidth, memory limits, and I/O policies. CPU quota limits available execution over a period and differs from CPU affinity. Hierarchical limits and shared ancestors affect actual capacity. Monitor throttling and pressure instead of relying only on host utilization.", "A group allowed 50 ms CPU per 100 ms period can exhaust its budget early with parallel workers and then wait, even while the host has idle cores.", "Budget|50 ms each 100 ms|Half-core average entitlement;Consume|Workers spend quota early|Budget exhausted;Throttle|Wait for replenishment|Latency spike possible", "Adding threads cannot increase a fixed CPU quota and may make burst throttling more visible.", "Why inspect ancestor limits?", "A child can be constrained by its parent's budget even if its own configuration appears generous."),
  "container-file-systems": T("Container images commonly use layered filesystems with immutable lower layers and a writable upper layer. Modifying a lower-layer file can trigger copy-up; deleting it can create a whiteout hiding the lower version. Volumes have separate lifecycle and performance properties. The writable container layer should not be confused with durable application storage.", "A container changes a large file inherited from its image. Copy-up can consume more space and I/O than the small logical edit suggests.", "Image|Large file in lower layer|Shared read-only backing;Modify|Copy file into upper layer|Additional I/O and space;Persist|Use appropriate volume for state|Explicit lifecycle", "Removing a file in a later image layer does not necessarily remove its bytes from earlier distributed layers.", "Why separate application data from the image?", "Data needs independent backup, persistence, permissions, and lifecycle management rather than replacement with each container instance."),
  "pid-1-and-signal-handling": T("The first process in a PID namespace has special lifecycle responsibilities, including handling adopted children and termination behavior. A wrapper shell may fail to forward signals or reap descendants correctly. Use an application or init arrangement that intentionally forwards termination, waits for children, and exits within the orchestrator's grace period.", "An entrypoint shell starts a worker without exec. Termination reaches the wrapper, while the worker may continue until forced shutdown.", "Entrypoint|Shell owns PID 1|Worker is child;Signal|Termination reaches wrapper|Forwarding required;Cleanup|Worker drains and parent reaps|Orderly exit", "Do not assume ordinary default signal behavior and reaping happen automatically for every PID 1 setup.", "Why use exec in a simple entrypoint?", "It replaces the wrapper with the application, making signal delivery and exit status more direct when no supervisor is required."),
  "container-resource-failures": T("Container failures can arise from CPU throttling, cgroup OOM, descriptor exhaustion, process-count limits, ephemeral storage, or network quotas. These limits may be reached while the host appears healthy. Diagnose with the container's effective limits, events, and workload timeline before changing application algorithms or host capacity.", "A deployment becomes slow only after concurrency doubles. CPU quota throttling rises while host CPU remains moderate, indicating a local budget bottleneck.", "Increase|Double request concurrency|Same resource limits;Observe|Throttle counters rise|Host still has spare CPU;Adjust|Bound concurrency or revise budget|Validate latency improvement", "Exit codes and restart counts are clues, not complete root causes; correlate them with resource events.", "Why can memory usage briefly overshoot expectations?", "Concurrent requests, buffers, allocator retention, and page cache accounting can all contribute beyond the application's nominal live-object estimate."),
  "container-security": T("Container security combines image provenance, least privilege, restricted capabilities, namespace configuration, syscall policy, filesystem permissions, and host maintenance. Running without unnecessary privileges reduces reachable attack surface. Shared-kernel isolation means a kernel vulnerability can cross the intended boundary, and mounted host resources can deliberately bypass much of that isolation.", "A service needs read-only configuration and one writable data directory. Give those narrow mounts rather than broad host filesystem access.", "Image|Use reviewed minimal runtime|Known dependencies;Runtime|Drop unnecessary authority|Restricted mounts and capabilities;Operate|Update and observe violations|Maintain boundary", "Privileged mode or a sensitive host socket can defeat the isolation expected from an ordinary container.", "Why is non-root useful but insufficient?", "It reduces some authority, but capabilities, mounts, credentials, kernel flaws, and application permissions still determine what the process can do."),
  "a-systematic-debugging-method": T("Start with a measurable symptom, establish its scope and timeline, then form a falsifiable hypothesis. Separate CPU execution, runnable waiting, blocking, and remote dependencies. Collect a minimal set of correlated observations, change one factor, and check whether the result supports the hypothesis. Preserve baseline data for comparison.", "A p99 regression begins after deployment. Compare request mix and per-stage timing before blaming the kernel or increasing every resource.", "Symptom|p99 rose after deploy|Define affected requests;Hypothesis|More time waiting for storage|Choose confirming metrics;Experiment|Revert or isolate changed path|Compare against baseline", "Changing several limits at once may improve performance without identifying the cause.", "What makes a good hypothesis?", "It predicts an observable difference and suggests an experiment whose outcome could prove it wrong."),
  "investigating-processes": T("Process investigation connects identities, parent-child relationships, state, elapsed time, credentials, and resources. ps and process filesystem metadata help locate the relevant worker rather than assuming the visible parent does all work. Distinguish a sleeping task from a dead process and correlate snapshots with sustained behavior.", "A service launcher is idle while child workers consume CPU. Inspect the process tree and per-thread activity before profiling the launcher.", "Locate|Find service parent|Entry point only;Expand|Inspect descendants and threads|Actual workers;Correlate|Match request and resource activity|Choose target for tracing", "A single process-state snapshot can miss brief running or blocked intervals.", "Why inspect threads separately?", "One hot or blocked thread can dominate latency while aggregate process statistics hide the imbalance."),
  "investigating-cpu": T("CPU diagnosis distinguishes useful user work, kernel work, runnable queueing, interrupt load, and container or VM scheduling constraints. Profile hot paths and correlate utilization with throughput. High load average can include non-CPU waiting on some systems, and one saturated core can bottleneck a process while total machine utilization looks low.", "On an eight-core machine, one event-loop thread consumes a full core while overall utilization is roughly one eighth. More idle cores do not automatically help that serial path.", "Aggregate|Machine appears lightly loaded|Eight-core denominator;Thread view|One thread saturated|Serial bottleneck;Profile|Find dominant callback|Reduce or offload work", "Do not infer spare application capacity from host-wide CPU percentage alone.", "When would adding cores help?", "When work can actually run in parallel and is not constrained by locks, quotas, memory bandwidth, or a serial stage."),
  "investigating-memory": T("Memory diagnosis separates reserved address space, resident private data, shared pages, file cache, swap, and allocator retention. Observe trends under a repeatable workload and after quiescence. Correlate faults and reclaim stalls with latency. A leak means retained resources grow without intended reuse or release, not merely that RSS is large.", "Run repeated identical batches and compare private memory after each settles. A steadily increasing retained baseline is stronger leak evidence than a one-time warm-cache increase.", "Baseline|Record private and shared usage|Before batch;Load|Run identical workload|Peak grows temporarily;Settle|Compare retained baseline|Leak or bounded reuse", "Garbage collection or free does not guarantee immediate RSS reduction.", "What evidence separates cache growth from a leak?", "Bounded reuse, eviction behavior, ownership profiles, and stable post-load baselines support intentional caching rather than unbounded retention."),
  "investigating-disk": T("Disk diagnosis combines application I/O demand with filesystem space, inode availability, device queueing, latency, and errors. A full filesystem can fail writes even when the device is fast; inode exhaustion can prevent file creation despite free data blocks. Network and layered filesystems add dependencies beyond the local block device.", "A service cannot create tiny files although free bytes remain. Check inode availability and directory growth before replacing the disk.", "Failure|Create returns space-related error|Not necessarily byte capacity;Inspect|Compare blocks and inode availability|Find exhausted resource;Repair|Manage file lifecycle|Verify creation succeeds", "Deleting active open files may not immediately reclaim space; identify remaining references.", "Why inspect both df-style and file-level data?", "Filesystem allocation includes objects and metadata not fully represented by a simple sum of visible file sizes."),
  "investigating-system-calls": T("Syscall tracing reveals interactions with kernel services, including arguments, results, and elapsed waits. A focused strace-style capture can identify repeated opens, failed connections, or long waits. It does not expose all user-space computation and can alter timing, so use short bounded captures and protect sensitive argument data.", "A request repeatedly opens the same configuration file. A syscall summary shows excessive open/read/close activity, suggesting a validated caching opportunity.", "Capture|Trace one worker briefly|Bounded sample;Group|Count calls and durations|Separate frequency from delay;Explain|Match calls to request path|Propose targeted change", "A process with few syscalls can still be CPU-bound in user space.", "Why compare elapsed syscall time with CPU time?", "Long elapsed waits may be blocked time rather than expensive computation inside the kernel."),
  "investigating-network-and-sockets": T("Socket investigation connects endpoints, owning processes, connection states, and queue sizes. ss or equivalent tools reveal listening and established sockets, while packet captures add wire timing. A large receive queue suggests the application is not draining data; a large send queue can reflect peer or path pressure, depending on protocol and state.", "A service's receive queue grows while packets arrive normally. Inspect application read progress and event-loop delay before blaming packet loss.", "Wire|Packets reach host|Network delivery observed;Socket|Receive queue accumulates|Application not draining;Application|Inspect reader and scheduling|Locate downstream stall", "Queue fields differ by socket state and platform; interpret the documented meaning.", "Why correlate socket and packet observations?", "Packets show transport events, while socket ownership and queues connect those events to the responsible local application."),
  "file-descriptor-exhaustion": T("Each open file, socket, pipe, and similar resource can consume a descriptor. Per-process limits and system-wide limits are distinct. A missing close on error or cancellation paths can leak descriptors gradually. Bound concurrent resources, use structured cleanup, and monitor counts by object type and lifetime.", "Each failed upstream request leaves one socket open. After enough failures, new accepts fail even though CPU and memory look normal.", "Failure path|Socket not closed|One leaked descriptor;Repeat|Count climbs toward limit|Capacity erodes;Exhaust|New open fails|Fix ownership and cleanup", "Simply increasing the descriptor limit only postpones failure when the leak remains.", "What test catches this class of bug?", "Repeat success, failure, timeout, and cancellation paths while asserting descriptor counts return near baseline after cleanup."),
  "profiling-and-flame-graphs": T("Sampling profilers estimate where execution spends time by collecting stacks. Flame-graph width represents sample weight, not chronological duration of one call; vertical position shows call ancestry. CPU profiles emphasize on-CPU work, while off-CPU analysis is needed for waiting. Sampling bias and symbol quality affect interpretation.", "A wide JSON serialization stack suggests substantial sampled CPU work. Optimize only after confirming it belongs to the latency-critical workload.", "Sample|Collect stacks over interval|Representative workload;Aggregate|Group common call paths|Width equals sample weight;Validate|Change hot path and remeasure|Confirm user-visible benefit", "A tall stack is not necessarily expensive, and an absent wait does not prove no blocking occurred.", "Why distinguish CPU and wall-clock profiles?", "A request can spend most elapsed time waiting while consuming little CPU; the profiles answer different questions."),
  "ebpf-and-bpftrace": T("eBPF supports constrained programs attached to kernel and other supported events, with verification and controlled helper interfaces. bpftrace provides a higher-level tracing language for many observability tasks. Event selection, aggregation, privileges, kernel support, and probe overhead matter. Prefer bounded aggregate measurements over emitting every event on a busy system.", "Count syscall occurrences by process during a short observation window, then narrow to the unexpectedly active worker for deeper analysis.", "Attach|Select relevant probe|Kernel support checked;Aggregate|Count events by identity|Bounded state;Detach|Report and stop probe|Limit observation cost", "Verification does not make every probe operationally free or every measurement interpretation correct.", "Why aggregate inside the tracing path?", "It reduces output volume and user-space transfer overhead, but map cardinality and aggregation keys must still be bounded."),
};
